WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.581 --> 00:00:03.331
(dramatic music)

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.263
- So ultimately I want to make the point

00:00:11.263 --> 00:00:14.630
that in such a world, growth and equality

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:17.500
are not only, they're not substitutes,

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.900
they are strong compliments.

00:00:26.501 --> 00:00:28.690
- So welcome to the 30th
Kuznets Memorial Lecture

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:30.610
presented by the Economic Growth Center.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.210
I'm Rohini Pande, I'm a professor

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.290
at the Economics Department
and director of EGC.

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:37.300
Because this lecture is an annual lecture

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.610
on issues in economic development

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:42.800
given by prominent economists
in memory of Simon Kuznets,

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.870
the famous Belarusian-American economist.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.600
Kuznets was not a Yale professor,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.933
but he was instrumental in founding EGC.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.890
In 1934, Kuznets helped launch

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.060
the US national income statistics series.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.373
the motivation was to use data for policy.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:00.150
The series was issued to meet the need

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.800
to describe consistently and in detail,

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.570
the economic tool taken
by the Great Depression.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:07.490
Post World War II, as
colonialism, the big question was

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.000
what would drive economic development

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.800
in the poorer parts of the world?

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.840
'Cause that's our reason
for a center to do something

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.280
that its home institution
were not able to,

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.050
to use data to describe development.

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:21.540
And so he turned to Yale
to set up the institute,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:22.800
which is now known as EGC.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.030
EGC was arguably the first
development research center

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.000
in a major US university that focused

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.430
on using quantitative methods

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.180
to understand economic development.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.500
Today, as we gain access to greater data

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.140
and improve computation
power and estimation methods,

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.100
economists are responding
to Kuznets' call of 1934,

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.300
which was to say the welfare
of a nation can scarcely

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.330
be inferred from a measurement
of national income alone,

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:49.270
to look at broader measures of inequality.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:50.950
This burning focus of development

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:51.870
research will be the theme

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.930
of Professor Goldberg's lecture tonight,

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:57.060
and of the conference that
she will lead tomorrow.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.490
When it comes to bridging
inequalities based on gender,

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.780
it takes bold women to be
willing to be the first

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.310
to enter spaces previously held by men.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.430
Penny Goldberg is one such woman.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.820
She was the first female
professor to be given tenure

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.830
by the Yale economic department.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.680
She was the first female editor-in-chief

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.510
of the American Economic Review.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:18.160
And as president-elect of the
Econometrics Society for 2021,

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.930
she will be its first female president.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.450
More recently, Penny served
as only the second ever

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.670
female chief economist of the
World Bank after Ann Krueger,

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:28.760
who also happened to
be the only other woman

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.290
to have delivered the Kuznets lecture

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:31.543
in its 30-year history.

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:34.440
Penny's research takes
on important questions

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:35.850
in trade and development.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.110
She studied last trade
liberalization episodes

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:39.450
in developing countries.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.730
Her work has drawn out the
contrasting implications

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.640
of trade reform in India and Latin America

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.910
for product markets and inequality.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.950
Her most recent research
examines the resurgence

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:50.240
of protectionism in the
US and the interplay

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.160
between informality and
trade liberalization.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.690
Penny is someone whose work
I've admired for a long time

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:57.660
and she has been an
important role model for me

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.720
and many other women in the profession.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.110
So this year as Yale
celebrates the 50th anniversary

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:04.400
of the matriculation of
women in Yale College,

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.920
and the 150th anniversary
of the first women students

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.450
in the university, it seems
particularly appropriate

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.410
that we welcome this
pioneering woman to deliver

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:13.340
the 13th Kuznets Memorial Lecture.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.081
Please welcome Professor Goldberg.

00:03:15.081 --> 00:03:18.248
(audience applauding)

00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:23.460
- Hello, first of all,
Rohini thank you so much

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.003
for this very generous introduction.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.303
I'm very happy to be back, for good now.

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:34.980
Yale has been a wonderful
home to me for many years,

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:39.170
and I'm looking forward to
assuming back my academic duties.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:42.740
But today I'm going to talk
still, as chief economist

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:47.493
of the World Bank Group, I have
three more days on my term.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:52.330
And the reason I mentioned that is not

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:54.598
because there's going
to be any juicy gossip.

00:03:54.598 --> 00:03:56.760
If that's what you're here for

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.980
I'm afraid I'm going to disappoint you.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.453
But because When picking
the topic for this lecture,

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.360
I did try to be consistent with the theme

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:11.830
of Kuznet's research,
which as Rohini mentioned,

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:15.320
was very much focused on
inequality and growth.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:19.240
But also I wanted to build
on my World Bank experience

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:20.480
for the past two years.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.040
So I'm going to draw on this.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.550
And what this means is I will start

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:32.020
with some preliminary
thoughts about the perspective

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.892
that one has from the chief economist's

00:04:34.892 --> 00:04:36.413
Office of the World Bank.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.750
What I mean by that, so
one advantage of being

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:43.800
at the World Bank is that one gets exposed

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.480
to what I would call the big
picture questions in economics.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:52.270
And for the past few years,
there has been a tendency

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:53.920
to focus on smaller questions,

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.950
at least in research in economics,

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:58.260
questions that we can address very well,

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.683
but the scope of them
tends to be more narrow.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.260
Perhaps many of you have seen
a very well known lecture

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:09.740
by one of the Nobel Prize
winners this year, Esther Duflo.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.760
She gave the lecture Ely few years ago

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.390
when the title of the Ely Lecture

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:15.600
was The Economist as a Plumber.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.650
And there she made the point
that it's very important

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.893
for economists to get the
details of implementation,

00:05:20.893 --> 00:05:22.920
of policy implementation right

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.290
because it doesn't matter
how good your policy is,

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.220
if you don't get the details right,

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:29.120
the policy is going to come to nothing.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.900
I love this lecture, I very
much believe in this message.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.990
And after being at the World
Bank for for a year and a half,

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:39.510
I believe in this message even
more strongly than before.

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:42.350
That said, I think one should not forget

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:45.250
that in order to build
a functional building,

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:47.670
you need the plumber, but
you also need the architect

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:48.860
and the engineer.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.755
And as much as I believe that some of us

00:05:50.755 --> 00:05:53.800
should spend time on
implementation and plumbing,

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:56.910
I also think some of us
should also spend some time

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:58.880
on the big picture questions in economics

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.810
and trying to figure out how
everything fits together.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:03.350
And the point, in the World Bank,

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:05.657
you are forced to do that all the time,

00:06:05.657 --> 00:06:07.955
partly because you
interact with countries,

00:06:07.955 --> 00:06:10.840
with finance ministers, with policymakers.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:13.090
And they really want more from you

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:15.570
than how a specific project will work,

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:19.620
they will want to know what
your vision for development is.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:23.800
Which brings me to one of the
motivations of this lecture.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.580
I'm going to make the point
that we're at a point in time

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:29.310
where we need a new vision,
a new model for development.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.930
I will explain what I mean in a moment.

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:35.170
So I think that was one very
positive aspect of being

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:36.400
at the World Bank, you're forced to think

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:38.450
about these questions.

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:41.520
The other thing that's very
different in the World Bank

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:45.700
compared to academia, is that
you are constantly forced

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:47.790
to think in cross country terms.

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:52.550
And as I'm sure you all know,
cross country regressions

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:56.853
have become an anathema in a
political work in academics.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:00.970
Many people don't take
them seriously anymore.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:03.990
But in the World Bank, this
is often the foundation

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.510
of giving advice to countries.

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.590
So countries will often
come to you and say,

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:11.560
you are the World Bank, you've
been to all these countries,

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:14.110
you know what works in country A,

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:19.110
can't you tell us how we could
apply it to our own country.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:21.640
So you're constantly forced to think

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:23.010
in this cross country terms,

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:26.470
to run cross country regressions
in your head all the time.

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.680
They actually think that
this is very productive,

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.000
which is a good thing to do
as long as you understand

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.780
the limitations of this and you don't use,

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:39.290
you don't confine yourself to
a straitjacket of regression,

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:41.982
and pretend that you have

00:07:41.982 --> 00:07:44.720
absolutely robust scientific evidence.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.270
So I mentioned that because
what I'm going to talk about

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:51.420
is going to be built on these
observations in some sense.

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:54.893
I'm going to employ some
cross country analysis.

00:07:56.126 --> 00:07:59.330
And I'm going to rely
a lot on correlations

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:02.110
rather than clean identification,

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:03.960
but I think some correlations

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:06.460
can be actually quite informative.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:08.410
So I'll use, this is not going to be

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:09.860
the standard academic talk.

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:12.590
I'm going to use many
pictures and correlations

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:15.223
to just tell a story.

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:21.310
So as I said, I think
there is great demand

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:25.930
among policymakers these
days for a new vision

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:28.750
for development that I'll be
more specific in a moment.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:32.640
At the same time, there is great demand

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:35.050
especially among advanced economies,

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:38.300
among industrialized
countries for more equality

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:40.430
or less inequality.

00:08:40.430 --> 00:08:43.300
So the focus of policy in many countries

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:47.690
in the last few years has been
on inequality not on poverty.

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:50.750
Poverty seems almost a forgotten word

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.540
outside the World Bank.

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:57.190
And whenever in the world
ranking, in talks or seminars,

00:08:57.190 --> 00:08:59.990
I made a point for more equality,

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:03.450
I immediately got pushback from countries,

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:06.270
from poor countries that ask,

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:08.380
does this mean you give up on growth?

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:10.050
We don't care about inequality,

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:12.580
for us growth is much more important.

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:14.273
And what underlies this comment

00:09:14.273 --> 00:09:17.360
is this very strongly
held view among economists

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.400
that there is a trade off
between growth and inequality.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.630
And of course, this trade
off is to a certain extent

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:25.380
based on the famous
Kuznets curve that posited

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:26.620
that there is a trade off at least

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:28.620
in the early stages of development,

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.590
there is a trade off between
growth and inequality

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:33.830
as an economy grows.

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:37.740
Then inequality rises, but
eventually it tapers off.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:42.320
So I will have something
to say about that too.

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:46.320
So, with that introduction,
let me give you

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:49.240
a more specific roadmap for this talk.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:54.140
I will start by explaining
what I mean by the old model

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:57.990
of development that was
based to a large extent

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:00.430
on export led industrialization.

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:01.314
This is a model, perhaps it
has been not that influential

00:10:01.314 --> 00:10:05.490
in academia, but it has,

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:08.980
it was the cornerstone of
advice that the World Bank

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:10.910
or the IMF and many other institutions,

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:13.100
international institutions gave.

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:16.350
Then I will talk about the recent retreat

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:19.840
from globalization and multilateralism

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:21.490
we've experienced in the last few years.

00:10:21.490 --> 00:10:23.690
and I will make the
point that this retreat

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:27.200
actually poses a challenge
for this old model.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:29.750
And that also gives rise to this need

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:31.810
for a new vision for development.

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:36.410
And regarding this new vision,
there are various proposals

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:38.590
that are made fairly casually actually

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:40.310
in the development world.

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:43.150
One of them is leapfrogging
into the service sector,

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:44.870
skipping the manufacturing stage.

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:48.260
Another one concerns
urbanization led development.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:50.730
And finally, what I really
want to talk about today

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:54.360
is the idea that if we
transition to a world

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:57.620
that's more closed, less
open with less trade,

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:00.630
with less globalization,
then domestic markets

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:01.760
become more important.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:04.640
And if domestic markets
become more important,

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:06.400
then a certain degree of equality

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:08.920
may become an important
precondition for growth.

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:10.723
So ultimately, I want to make the point

00:11:10.723 --> 00:11:12.430
that in such a world,

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:16.560
growth and equality are not
only, they're not substitutes,

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:18.193
they are strong complements.

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:23.530
So when I make this point, of
course, the way I express it,

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:28.220
it's very general and open ended.

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:32.530
So I will try to make it more
concrete by operationalizing

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:36.570
this notion of the domestic
market and to do that,

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:39.260
that's when you need a
conceptual framework.

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:41.800
So I will talk a little bit
about the theoretical framework

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:43.580
that one can use to
think about these issues

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:45.660
and then some empirical work

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:49.740
that I've been recently working
on and present some results.

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:52.860
And eventually, as I said,
the point I want to make

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.320
and this will be the conclusion in case

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:57.573
you fall asleep or you
have to leave early.

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:02.370
For a small car, it's nearly impossible

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:05.293
if not completely impossible
to eliminate poverty

00:12:05.293 --> 00:12:07.690
if this country remains closed.

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:12.510
So openness to trade
is extremely important.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:17.540
So, when we mean openness in that context,

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:19.870
openness these days means more than trade,

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:21.480
it means deep integration.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:25.500
So with deep integration, I
mean, essentially measures

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:27.870
that make sure that
whatever trade agreements

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:30.890
you have are enforceable,
but also measures

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:35.890
that contribute to stronger capital

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:39.997
and labor flows between countries.

00:12:39.997 --> 00:12:44.710
Alternatively, if you don't have this path

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:47.480
that involves more openness, more trade,

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:49.990
and if you need to focus
on the domestic market,

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:51.580
larger countries have it easier.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:53.300
If you're in India, you're going to have

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:55.240
an easier time growing because you control

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:56.610
the domestic market.

00:12:56.610 --> 00:13:01.360
But in such a setting,
it's important to have

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:03.510
a certain degree of
equality that will support

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:06.420
the emergence of a healthy middle class,

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:08.500
so that the purchasing
power of this middle class

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:10.684
can support development.

00:13:10.684 --> 00:13:13.350
So this is where I'm going with this.

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:17.310
And let me now be a little more specific.

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:20.010
So as I said, I will start by explaining

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:25.010
what the old model of export-led
industrialization has been.

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:29.060
So what's this model?

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:33.830
So this model is part of the
so called Washington Consensus.

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:38.460
And these terms have become
extremely controversial

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:40.320
but actually, if you think about

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.960
what the Washington Consensus involved,

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:47.960
it involved a set of many
policies, a set of policies,

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:51.900
many of which actually
are fairly reasonable

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:54.260
from an economist point of view.

00:13:54.260 --> 00:13:56.970
Three of the most important pillars

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:00.230
of this Washington
Consensus, were deregulation,

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:02.610
privatization and trade liberalization.

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:06.170
So embracing open international markets.

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:09.180
And the idea there was as follows.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:12.190
So, if we apply this model
to developing countries,

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:15.890
developing countries are
abundant in low skilled labor,

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:17.290
low wage labor.

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:20.841
So by engaging in free
trade, in open trade,

00:14:20.841 --> 00:14:25.570
they can utilize their
comparative advantage.

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:28.270
And this comparative advantage is most,

00:14:28.270 --> 00:14:30.913
can be most utilized
in basic manufacturing.

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:35.278
By exporting to higher income countries,

00:14:35.278 --> 00:14:36.830
a lot of good things happen.

00:14:36.830 --> 00:14:38.640
As I mentioned, they can utilize

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:40.160
their comparative advantage.

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:43.140
In addition, they can tap
into the purchasing power

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:44.240
of richer countries.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:49.240
If you were China in the 80s,
or Vietnam in the early 90s,

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:53.010
you don't need to rely
on a rich domestic market

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:54.240
to sell your products.

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:56.340
You can rely on the richer consumers

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.023
in the United States or Europe.

00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:02.160
Of course, you can
realize economies of scale

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:06.600
from selling to the global
market, and hence industrialize.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:08.890
And finally, you can use
all the export revenue

00:15:08.890 --> 00:15:13.670
to finance investments in
infrastructure or human capital.

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:16.370
So investments that are going
to give you the foundations

00:15:16.370 --> 00:15:18.483
for long run development.

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:24.620
So this kind of model
that has been applied

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:27.020
to many countries, mostly in East Asia

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:28.200
is logically consistent.

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:30.470
You can see how the various parts work.

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:33.830
By the way, here I'm
presenting a verbal feature

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:36.130
of these models,
international trade economists

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:39.140
have spent many years building models

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:41.083
that formalize these ideas.

00:15:42.500 --> 00:15:43.930
So the model has been disputed

00:15:43.930 --> 00:15:45.760
that it's logically consistent.

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:47.930
It's a framework that we understand.

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:50.420
You can disagree with it, but
once you have the framework,

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:53.360
you know how to attack
it and how to take issue

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:54.860
with some of these assumptions.

00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:58.700
But I would say overall, it
has been actually supported

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:01.190
by casual observer and evidence.

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:04.520
And here I'm thinking, of
course, mostly of what happened

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:06.720
in East Asia, especially China.

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:09.347
So let me give you the
story in a few pictures.

00:16:09.347 --> 00:16:12.570
And as I said, I'm going to
rely here on correlations.

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:15.270
So you're not going to
see any regressions,

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:17.540
claiming identification.

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:21.280
The modern era, so post World War II

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:24.230
and this starts in 1827, okay?

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:26.560
And on the vertical axis, our exports,

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:29.670
global exports as percentage of GDP.

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:31.770
So you can see, there is some variations

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:33.610
in this percentage over time.

00:16:33.610 --> 00:16:38.610
Here, that's 1945 and after 1945,

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:44.330
so after World War II, exports
start rising very fast.

00:16:44.440 --> 00:16:46.960
This year, this is the
period of what people call

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:51.340
the hyperglobalisation, when
trade starts growing very fast

00:16:51.340 --> 00:16:53.640
as a percentage of GDP.

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:56.160
And this is partly
powered by the emergence

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:57.667
of global value chains

00:16:57.667 --> 00:17:02.143
and the emergence of
fragmentation in production.

00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:05.783
So this is a global phenomenon,

00:17:05.783 --> 00:17:07.910
it's not driven by specific countries.

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:10.500
So here you can see
essentially the same picture.

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:12.503
So here, fortunately,
the numbers came out.

00:17:12.503 --> 00:17:15.600
And you can see for the
world, all this is essentially

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:17.150
the previous graph.

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:19.186
But you can also see it
for selected countries.

00:17:19.186 --> 00:17:22.740
And what's interesting
here, I want to point out

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:27.000
is if you look at the USA,
this is the new blue line here.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:29.520
You see it trend upwards, but for the US,

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:32.000
this trend is not that pronounced.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:33.780
And the reason, part of the reason is

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:36.640
the US is already a very
large, rich country.

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:38.740
So trade is important for the US.

00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:42.200
But it's not as important for
the US as it is for countries

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:43.743
that are still poor.

00:17:43.743 --> 00:17:45.640
It's of course, very important for China.

00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:49.730
This is China, and many
people say hyperglobalization,

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:51.900
the increasing trade is driven by China.

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:54.640
China is clearly an
important part of the story.

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:57.700
But it's not just China,
it's many other countries.

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:01.883
For example, it's India,
that's the green line here.

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:05.850
So here I put some of
the biggest countries.

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:08.940
But you can see also more
clearly in this graph

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:11.410
that shows the composition
of world exports

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:12.410
by income groups.

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:17.190
And this is based on a classification

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:18.450
that the World Bank uses.

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:20.690
The World Bank makes a distinction

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:22.310
between high income countries

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:25.820
and then between upper middle
income, lower middle income

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:27.370
and low income countries.

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:30.170
And this classification
here is based on 1987.

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:32.162
So it's time invariant.

00:18:32.162 --> 00:18:36.690
So for example, China was a
low income country in '87.

00:18:36.690 --> 00:18:38.660
By now it's upper middle income.

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:40.420
But for the purpose of this graph,

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:43.860
China will always appear
in the low income category.

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:47.450
So you can see in '85,
if we take countries

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:49.170
other than the high income countries,

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:53.260
cumulatively, they accounted
for about 13% of total exports,

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:55.870
world exports, and that makes sense.

00:18:55.870 --> 00:18:59.070
Most of the economic activity
comes from the rich countries

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:00.430
as you would expect.

00:19:00.430 --> 00:19:05.430
So by 2015, if you take
the cumulative share

00:19:07.577 --> 00:19:11.650
of upper middle income,
lower middle income

00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:15.730
and low income countries is about 33%.

00:19:15.730 --> 00:19:18.830
So it has increased
dramatically, and that part

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:20.760
is driven to a very large extent by China.

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:22.530
But again, it's not only China,

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:24.358
many other developing countries

00:19:24.358 --> 00:19:27.240
have participated in this phenomenon.

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:32.240
Now, what has this done for
poverty across the world?

00:19:32.650 --> 00:19:36.120
At the same time, we had
a very sharp reduction

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:41.120
in global poverty and global
inequality across the world.

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:43.140
And of course, you're
going to say that there

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:45.910
are many factors that drove this decrease.

00:19:45.910 --> 00:19:47.010
It's not just trade,

00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:50.020
and perhaps the most
important one was peace.

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:53.265
You cannot have growth and
prosperity and poverty reduction

00:19:53.265 --> 00:19:56.690
unless you have peace,
and that's certainly true.

00:19:56.690 --> 00:19:58.890
But I will also make the point that trade

00:19:58.890 --> 00:20:00.730
has played an important role.

00:20:00.730 --> 00:20:02.620
And this is not something
that you can show

00:20:02.620 --> 00:20:05.730
through a regression
that is going to address

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:09.760
all concerns you may have
regarding identification.

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:13.630
But I do think that if
we take the evidence

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:15.640
that has been accumulated over the years

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:18.930
and by that I mean regressions,
I mean anecdotal evidence

00:20:18.930 --> 00:20:20.390
and anecdotal accounts.

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:23.510
I mean that many case
studies that actually

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:25.802
my predecessor at the
World Bank, Ann Krueger,

00:20:25.802 --> 00:20:29.210
conducted 30 years ago,
mostly on East Asia

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:30.493
and Southeast Asia.

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:34.363
Then all these studies
together tell the story

00:20:34.363 --> 00:20:39.363
of trade contributing to
growth and poverty reduction.

00:20:39.710 --> 00:20:41.320
Not to mention the recent experience,

00:20:41.320 --> 00:20:43.600
the relatively recent experience of China

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:45.513
and Vietnam and Korea.

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:49.643
And this story is also
reflected in a series of books.

00:20:49.643 --> 00:20:52.180
So I will mention a few here.

00:20:52.180 --> 00:20:54.850
Deaton's "The Great Escape".

00:20:54.850 --> 00:20:57.850
So this is a book that's extremely
critical of the World Bank

00:20:59.260 --> 00:21:00.730
but it does make the point

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:05.280
that globalization has
contributed very significantly

00:21:06.230 --> 00:21:08.320
to the great escape, and the great escape

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:10.920
is the great escape from poverty.

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:14.610
There is the World Bank report,
world Development Report

00:21:14.610 --> 00:21:18.750
from 2006 and that report
also made the point,

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:21.540
the very important point at that time,

00:21:21.540 --> 00:21:25.920
that if we think about
inequality, not within borders,

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:29.080
but globally about the difference,

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:33.370
the discrepancy between the wellbeing

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:36.923
of the globally rich
and the globally poor,

00:21:36.923 --> 00:21:41.190
this has been dramatically
reduced post World War II.

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:43.100
And one of the persons who worked

00:21:43.100 --> 00:21:44.720
on that world development report

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:47.140
was actually Branko Milanovic,

00:21:47.140 --> 00:21:49.456
who after he left the World
Bank, he actually worked

00:21:49.456 --> 00:21:52.860
on the book of his book,
"Global Inequality".

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:54.570
So the book became a bestseller

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:55.977
and it made him truly famous.

00:21:55.977 --> 00:21:58.844
And that's again a book
that makes this point

00:21:58.844 --> 00:22:00.762
that global inequality,

00:22:00.762 --> 00:22:02.990
by global inequality, in some sense

00:22:02.990 --> 00:22:05.500
you can take that as a synonym for poverty

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:07.887
has been reduced dramatically
post World War II.

00:22:07.887 --> 00:22:10.040
And again globalization has played

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:12.483
a very important role in all this.

00:22:13.460 --> 00:22:15.425
And that raises also the question

00:22:15.425 --> 00:22:19.340
that's actually a question
that Branko Milanovic

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:21.820
explicitly asked in his book,

00:22:21.820 --> 00:22:24.100
whether there is actually a trade off

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:28.430
between global inequality and
within country inequality.

00:22:28.430 --> 00:22:31.510
So one of his hypotheses
is that this reduction

00:22:31.510 --> 00:22:34.150
in global inequality has
happened at the expense

00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:39.150
of an increase in domestic inequality.

00:22:41.070 --> 00:22:43.803
Some more pictures that tell this story.

00:22:45.046 --> 00:22:46.330
And I think these are very telling.

00:22:46.330 --> 00:22:49.443
This is the very famous elephant curve.

00:22:51.580 --> 00:22:54.640
So the blue part comes from Branko,

00:22:54.640 --> 00:22:57.173
from actually Branko
Milanovic beats and Lakner.

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:02.480
Actually the green part was later added

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:04.137
by Carol Freund.

00:23:05.770 --> 00:23:08.960
So what this elephant, so I will call this

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:12.010
the old elephant curve
because it has been updated.

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:13.533
I'll show you in a moment how.

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:16.900
But what this elephant curve shows

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:20.050
is on the horizontal axis,
you have the percentiles

00:23:20.050 --> 00:23:22.190
of the global income distribution.

00:23:22.190 --> 00:23:25.880
So this is taking the world's population,

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:28.660
putting it together, and then
looking at the percentiles

00:23:28.660 --> 00:23:30.470
of this distribution.

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:32.100
And then on the vertical axis,

00:23:32.100 --> 00:23:34.501
you have the income growth rate.

00:23:34.501 --> 00:23:38.010
In percentage terms, this
is per capita income.

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:42.813
And this is all between
'88 and 2008, okay?

00:23:43.740 --> 00:23:47.590
So precisely this period
includes precisely

00:23:47.590 --> 00:23:49.560
the period of the hyperglobalization.

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:53.100
It is during this period, where
you see the fastest growth

00:23:53.100 --> 00:23:55.090
in these percentiles.

00:23:55.090 --> 00:23:57.160
These are actually the world's poor.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:24:01.490
So, according to that figure,
it's mostly the world's poor

00:24:01.490 --> 00:24:04.773
that have benefited the most from growth.

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:08.780
Of course, the very
rich also benefit a lot.

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:13.780
So the very top 1%, but
this is the main message

00:24:14.600 --> 00:24:18.050
of this curve was that poverty,

00:24:18.050 --> 00:24:21.727
that the growth was such that
actually poverty declined.

00:24:21.727 --> 00:24:24.440
It declined very sharply.

00:24:24.440 --> 00:24:27.260
Now, as I said this curve was updated

00:24:27.260 --> 00:24:31.690
with very different data
recently by a number,

00:24:31.690 --> 00:24:35.890
by a team of researchers,
three of whom in that team

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:40.507
include the famous trio,
(mumbles) and Sukwon of Berkeley.

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:46.070
And the reason I mention
that is because this curve

00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:49.710
uses different data, so it
uses tax, income tax data,

00:24:49.710 --> 00:24:52.580
so the same data they have
used in their other work

00:24:52.580 --> 00:24:55.110
to demonstrate that it's the top 1%

00:24:55.110 --> 00:24:57.424
that has benefited the most

00:24:57.424 --> 00:25:02.424
from recent economic changes.

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:06.490
And this curve does not look
like an elephant anymore.

00:25:06.490 --> 00:25:09.440
I mean it looks more
like a dragon, I think.

00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:13.270
But what it shows very clearly

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:17.323
is that you have a
disproportionate increase in,

00:25:20.400 --> 00:25:22.472
well being at the top 1%.

00:25:22.472 --> 00:25:27.472
So here, the growth incident
shows that it's the top 1%

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:31.723
that captured a very large
share of total growth.

00:25:33.100 --> 00:25:36.930
However, it also makes
very clear the point

00:25:36.930 --> 00:25:39.980
that many have made that
it's the middle class

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:42.650
in the US and Western
Europe that gets squeezed.

00:25:42.650 --> 00:25:45.680
Okay, so these are the
people who benefit the least.

00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:50.090
But still, you see that the
really poor in the world

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:51.870
still benefit from growth.

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:55.970
So the message that the
recent economic trends

00:25:55.970 --> 00:25:59.490
have benefited the poor I
think remains still valid,

00:25:59.490 --> 00:26:01.990
even if you use this updated curve

00:26:01.990 --> 00:26:05.530
that paints a much less rosy picture

00:26:05.530 --> 00:26:08.360
about what growth does for the poor.

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:11.230
And by the way, if you
wonder what the short

00:26:11.230 --> 00:26:13.540
of the differences
between the two curves are

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:16.670
that there are many, in
my view, the main issue

00:26:16.670 --> 00:26:19.825
is that this curve is based on tax data

00:26:19.825 --> 00:26:22.800
that captured the very
top of the distribution.

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:25.037
The other curve that I showed
you is based on survey data

00:26:25.037 --> 00:26:28.340
and there you always have the
problem that the top incomes

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:30.550
are not captured by these surveys.

00:26:30.550 --> 00:26:32.423
But again, my point here is that,

00:26:33.370 --> 00:26:37.290
any way you put it, it's
very hard to deny the message

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:39.377
that poverty has very sharply declined.

00:26:39.377 --> 00:26:43.010
And it is also reflected
in this picture here,

00:26:43.010 --> 00:26:46.880
which comes from World
Bank estimates of poverty.

00:26:46.880 --> 00:26:50.820
So what this shows is what
has happened to poverty,

00:26:50.820 --> 00:26:55.040
to the headcount, to the
poverty headcount over time

00:26:55.040 --> 00:26:57.010
by different regions.

00:26:57.010 --> 00:26:58.853
So first, if you look at the total,

00:26:59.740 --> 00:27:03.370
you see that the total
has, we are here now,

00:27:03.370 --> 00:27:06.880
the total has declined so the
number of poor has declined.

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:09.180
This is the projection
according to the World Bank

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:12.043
what is likely to happen by 2030.

00:27:13.180 --> 00:27:15.470
One interesting aspect
of this graph is actually

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:18.380
that it's broken down by different regions

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:21.150
and the classifications of
the regions again reflects

00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:23.010
the World Bank classification.

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:25.490
So, there are some
interesting patterns here

00:27:25.490 --> 00:27:28.110
and one pattern is let me point out,

00:27:28.110 --> 00:27:33.110
this part, the brown
one, this is East Asia.

00:27:35.630 --> 00:27:38.120
In East Asia, you can see that poverty

00:27:38.120 --> 00:27:40.103
has been almost eliminated.

00:27:41.090 --> 00:27:43.700
This part that you can
hardly see, reddish,

00:27:43.700 --> 00:27:45.560
is Europe and Central Asia.

00:27:45.560 --> 00:27:47.230
And again, in these parts of the world,

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:49.228
poverty has been eliminated.

00:27:49.228 --> 00:27:52.210
By the way, I forgot
to mention poverty here

00:27:52.210 --> 00:27:56.790
is defined as living on
less than $1.9 per day.

00:27:58.640 --> 00:28:01.830
So it's a very low bar for poverty.

00:28:01.830 --> 00:28:04.810
So these people are
really the extremely poor,

00:28:04.810 --> 00:28:08.330
it's a very modest
criteria but nevertheless.

00:28:08.330 --> 00:28:12.348
Even using this criteria,
we see that if you look

00:28:12.348 --> 00:28:14.620
at a different region,
if you look at Africa,

00:28:14.620 --> 00:28:16.200
this is the yellow part.

00:28:16.200 --> 00:28:19.640
Here, the picture is much, much less rosy.

00:28:19.640 --> 00:28:22.110
Not only has poverty not declined,

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:25.010
poverty has increased
in Sub Saharan Africa.

00:28:25.010 --> 00:28:28.890
In fact, it's projected to
increase even more, okay?

00:28:28.890 --> 00:28:31.520
And the same actually applies
to this part that again,

00:28:31.520 --> 00:28:34.730
you can hardly see because
the region is very small.

00:28:34.730 --> 00:28:37.750
This is MENA, so this is
Middle East and North Africa.

00:28:37.750 --> 00:28:40.010
Again, poverty is increasing there.

00:28:40.010 --> 00:28:42.540
So poverty has declined in
many parts of the world,

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:47.160
but it remains very high in
Africa and the Middle East,

00:28:47.160 --> 00:28:49.710
and in fact, there it's increasing.

00:28:49.710 --> 00:28:54.710
So, this is in terms of,
in terms of background

00:28:54.720 --> 00:28:56.670
of what has happened to poverty.

00:28:56.670 --> 00:29:00.020
Now, the second point I want to make

00:29:00.020 --> 00:29:03.500
is that I don't know, if I
have managed to convince you

00:29:03.500 --> 00:29:06.010
that globalization was part of the story.

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:08.820
It's not the entire part,
there are many other things

00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:10.457
that happened at the same time.

00:29:10.457 --> 00:29:13.588
But we do see the fastest
poverty reductions

00:29:13.588 --> 00:29:15.910
in precisely those parts of the world

00:29:15.910 --> 00:29:18.200
that embrace openness and again,

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:22.130
East Asia comes immediately
to mind, especially China.

00:29:22.130 --> 00:29:26.970
But that said, is what we
have experienced since 2008,

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:30.430
is a slowdown in trade.

00:29:30.430 --> 00:29:34.453
So this slowdown started
after the financial crisis.

00:29:34.453 --> 00:29:38.970
So during the financial crisis,
trade collapsed completely.

00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:42.260
After the financial crisis,
it started growing back,

00:29:42.260 --> 00:29:44.650
but again at a very slow pace

00:29:44.650 --> 00:29:49.650
and it never quite gained
its previous momentum.

00:29:49.970 --> 00:29:53.770
And some people have
attributed this slow down

00:29:53.770 --> 00:29:56.290
to a secular slowdown.

00:29:56.290 --> 00:29:58.080
What I mean by that is the following.

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:00.870
So to a certain extent, trade was fueled

00:30:00.870 --> 00:30:03.330
by this emergence of global value chains.

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:04.940
And you could have global value chains

00:30:04.940 --> 00:30:07.780
partly because it became
technologically possible

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:09.440
to fragment production.

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:11.440
And many thought at
some point fragmentation

00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:12.600
may have run its course.

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:15.370
If you take a car, you
can split it into parts

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:18.220
and components, and you can
trade those back and forth.

00:30:18.220 --> 00:30:20.600
But eventually you run out
of parts to split, right?

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:24.610
You go down to the screws,
and the nuts and bolts,

00:30:24.610 --> 00:30:27.270
eventually you cannot fragment anymore.

00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:29.510
So there are some who have thought,

00:30:29.510 --> 00:30:32.223
who thought that this
may be part of the story.

00:30:33.485 --> 00:30:35.780
And in fact, one thing
you see is if you look

00:30:35.780 --> 00:30:39.277
at global value chains trade,
so it peaked around 2010.

00:30:39.277 --> 00:30:42.400
And then it started going
down as a percentage of trade.

00:30:42.400 --> 00:30:44.430
So again, with global value chain,

00:30:44.430 --> 00:30:45.900
this is the hyperglobalization.

00:30:45.900 --> 00:30:48.403
You see this very fast
growth during that time.

00:30:49.330 --> 00:30:52.780
In my view, there's very little evidence

00:30:52.780 --> 00:30:55.500
to support this view.

00:30:55.500 --> 00:30:58.710
There's something else that
has been going on mostly

00:30:58.710 --> 00:31:02.010
in very recent years, so
in the last five years.

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:05.140
And this is the very
strong policy backlash,

00:31:05.140 --> 00:31:08.470
so the backlash against globalization.

00:31:08.470 --> 00:31:12.920
And I think this presents
a much more serious threat

00:31:14.970 --> 00:31:18.693
to trade and openness and
globalization in general.

00:31:18.693 --> 00:31:20.480
So what do I mean by these?

00:31:20.480 --> 00:31:24.640
So first, there has been
a revolt of the public

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:27.540
in many developed primarily countries

00:31:27.540 --> 00:31:30.000
against free trade and immigration.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:34.980
And this is not specific
to a particular country

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:36.670
or to a particular party.

00:31:36.670 --> 00:31:40.140
We see it in the United States,
but if there is one issue,

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:43.040
whether it's bipartisan support for

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:45.100
is that the opposition to free trade.

00:31:45.100 --> 00:31:49.500
You can be Donald Trump or Bernie Sanders,

00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:54.500
and they both advocate protectionism.

00:31:57.429 --> 00:31:58.767
Even more important than the tariffs

00:31:58.767 --> 00:32:00.380
and the return to protectionism

00:32:00.380 --> 00:32:02.250
has been what the US has been doing

00:32:02.250 --> 00:32:03.710
to the World Trade Organization.

00:32:03.710 --> 00:32:06.850
So the World Trade
Organization is by now defunct

00:32:08.080 --> 00:32:11.700
due to the United States policies.

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:15.330
In the UK, we have had
Brexit, which again,

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:18.230
is a retreat from multilateralism.

00:32:18.230 --> 00:32:21.920
I already mentioned the World
Trade Organization, the WTO.

00:32:21.920 --> 00:32:25.440
In addition, despite
this generally held view

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:30.020
that trade is free, there has
been uneven liberalization

00:32:30.020 --> 00:32:31.210
of agricultural trade.

00:32:31.210 --> 00:32:33.590
Agricultural trade is
still very protected.

00:32:33.590 --> 00:32:36.380
And this is something
that's a very big issue

00:32:36.380 --> 00:32:38.020
for developing countries.

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:41.270
The services trade
remains highly restricted.

00:32:41.270 --> 00:32:45.620
And finally, they are arising
behind what people call,

00:32:45.620 --> 00:32:48.760
behind the border measures
and other distortions.

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:50.730
These are all on the rise.

00:32:50.730 --> 00:32:53.810
So if you take all these
together, I think it's fair to say

00:32:53.810 --> 00:32:58.470
that there has been a
retreat from globalization.

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:02.410
And let me show you,
again, in some pictures

00:33:02.410 --> 00:33:03.580
what the trends are.

00:33:03.580 --> 00:33:08.230
So this shows what has
happened to trade policy,

00:33:08.230 --> 00:33:12.340
not trade between 1948 and 2016.

00:33:12.340 --> 00:33:15.200
And I often use this
graph to make the point

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:17.758
that despite this, again broadly held view

00:33:17.758 --> 00:33:22.758
that globalization has been
mostly technological phenomenon

00:33:23.110 --> 00:33:27.530
fueled by low communication
and transportation costs,

00:33:27.530 --> 00:33:29.810
I want to make the
point that trade policy,

00:33:29.810 --> 00:33:31.600
policy has been important.

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:33.360
One way you see that is by seeing

00:33:33.360 --> 00:33:34.497
what happened directly to tariffs.

00:33:34.497 --> 00:33:36.520
So these are tariffs, applied tariffs

00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:37.840
in developing countries.

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:40.250
So these types have been going down.

00:33:40.250 --> 00:33:43.210
More importantly, if
you look at membership

00:33:43.210 --> 00:33:44.970
in the World Trade Organization.

00:33:44.970 --> 00:33:49.370
So this membership has gone up steadily.

00:33:49.370 --> 00:33:52.470
And especially, what was really important

00:33:52.470 --> 00:33:54.720
is that the World Trade Organization

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:56.980
included many developing countries.

00:33:56.980 --> 00:33:58.420
And perhaps even more importantly,

00:33:58.420 --> 00:34:00.760
we've had many regional trade agreements.

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:03.820
So these blue bars show the increase

00:34:03.820 --> 00:34:05.440
in regional trade agreements.

00:34:05.440 --> 00:34:07.720
So if you take all
these measures together;

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:11.760
lower tariffs, increase participation

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:15.680
in World Trade Organization,
in regional trade agreements,

00:34:15.680 --> 00:34:18.170
they show that, until 2016,

00:34:18.170 --> 00:34:22.490
they show a trend
towards more integration.

00:34:22.490 --> 00:34:24.591
So that is to be contrasted

00:34:24.591 --> 00:34:27.700
with what has happened post-2016.

00:34:27.700 --> 00:34:29.670
So this is the new policy environment.

00:34:29.670 --> 00:34:33.600
So this is the percentage of imports

00:34:33.600 --> 00:34:35.440
that's covered by tariffs.

00:34:35.440 --> 00:34:36.730
It's still a very small one.

00:34:36.730 --> 00:34:40.420
So, this is for the United
States, about 12% as of 2018.

00:34:40.420 --> 00:34:41.810
By now it's actually higher.

00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:43.693
China, Mexico and the EU.

00:34:45.050 --> 00:34:48.651
You may think this is still
a very low percentage,

00:34:48.651 --> 00:34:52.100
but what's important is
that it has risen relative

00:34:52.100 --> 00:34:54.550
to what it was five years ago.

00:34:54.550 --> 00:34:57.890
And it's also accompanied
by certain rhetoric

00:34:57.890 --> 00:35:00.923
that makes the future
of trade very uncertain.

00:35:01.880 --> 00:35:05.270
Another telling graph is this one.

00:35:05.270 --> 00:35:07.740
So it shows how many new trade agreements

00:35:07.740 --> 00:35:10.180
were formed in recent years.

00:35:10.180 --> 00:35:13.590
By 2018, this number had
dropped dramatically.

00:35:13.590 --> 00:35:14.980
This is partly due to the fact

00:35:14.980 --> 00:35:18.130
that there is enormous
uncertainty regarding the future

00:35:18.130 --> 00:35:19.370
of trade relations.

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:24.350
So the world is becoming
much more closed in my view

00:35:24.350 --> 00:35:27.880
and this is something
that I could not have said

00:35:27.880 --> 00:35:28.940
about a year ago.

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:33.510
When trade tensions
first arose, my view was

00:35:33.510 --> 00:35:38.510
that perhaps this was
just talk and negotiation.

00:35:40.940 --> 00:35:43.100
We'll have to wait and see what happens.

00:35:43.100 --> 00:35:46.513
But I think after two years of,

00:35:47.830 --> 00:35:52.130
of seeing what has
happened across the world,

00:35:52.130 --> 00:35:55.260
I do think we we are in a new regime,

00:35:55.260 --> 00:35:58.930
where trade tensions are
going to be the new normal.

00:35:58.930 --> 00:36:01.593
So this is on the trade
side, the policy side.

00:36:02.530 --> 00:36:04.703
So I think in such an environment,

00:36:05.850 --> 00:36:07.950
if you are a developing country,

00:36:07.950 --> 00:36:11.180
if you are a developing country
that has not yet benefited

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:13.670
from trade with the US or Europe,

00:36:13.670 --> 00:36:17.460
if you are a small country
in Africa, if you are Kenya,

00:36:17.460 --> 00:36:20.790
or if you are Ghana, I
don't think you can be sure

00:36:20.790 --> 00:36:22.640
that you can apply the same model

00:36:22.640 --> 00:36:25.920
of export led industrialization
that apply to China,

00:36:25.920 --> 00:36:29.540
I don't think you can bet on
this for your future growth.

00:36:29.540 --> 00:36:31.630
An additional challenge
is that many countries

00:36:31.630 --> 00:36:34.650
are confronted with new technologies.

00:36:34.650 --> 00:36:39.130
And there is a general fear that robots

00:36:39.130 --> 00:36:41.690
are going to make low wage labor

00:36:41.690 --> 00:36:44.233
in developing countries redundant.

00:36:45.090 --> 00:36:46.830
So remember, one of the big advantages

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:50.090
of developing countries has
always been that they can offer

00:36:51.810 --> 00:36:53.840
low wage labor.

00:36:53.840 --> 00:36:56.520
If low wage labor becomes less important

00:36:56.520 --> 00:36:59.870
because firms can rely on machines,

00:36:59.870 --> 00:37:01.830
then developing countries
are going to lose

00:37:01.830 --> 00:37:05.370
this disadvantage they have had.

00:37:05.370 --> 00:37:07.410
That said, I have to say that so far

00:37:07.410 --> 00:37:09.220
we have not seen any
evidence of (mumbles).

00:37:09.220 --> 00:37:12.120
So this is based actually on some work

00:37:12.120 --> 00:37:13.660
we did in the World Bank last year

00:37:13.660 --> 00:37:16.110
in the context of their
World Development Report.

00:37:16.110 --> 00:37:18.270
And let me just show you this graph

00:37:18.270 --> 00:37:20.860
that contradicts what
I just said actually.

00:37:20.860 --> 00:37:23.427
So what this graph shows is that,

00:37:23.427 --> 00:37:26.410
and again it's a correlation
for the most part.

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:31.250
The horizontal axis you
have industries are ordered

00:37:31.250 --> 00:37:33.900
according to the right
intensity of using robots.

00:37:33.900 --> 00:37:36.500
So for example, here
the automobile industry

00:37:36.500 --> 00:37:38.880
uses robots very intensively,

00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:42.660
followed by rubber and
plastics, metal, and so on.

00:37:42.660 --> 00:37:47.290
And so what we found is
that those industries

00:37:47.290 --> 00:37:51.000
that use robots very intensively
are actually the industries

00:37:51.000 --> 00:37:54.660
that also experience the
fastest growth of imports

00:37:54.660 --> 00:37:56.017
from developing countries.

00:37:56.017 --> 00:37:57.537
And these imports are imports

00:37:57.537 --> 00:38:00.070
in intermediate products and parts.

00:38:00.070 --> 00:38:02.780
Essentially, the story
behind this is that,

00:38:02.780 --> 00:38:05.153
by using robots, you
increase productivity.

00:38:06.212 --> 00:38:10.030
And as firms become more
productive and grow,

00:38:10.030 --> 00:38:14.510
they need more intermediate
parts, more components,

00:38:14.510 --> 00:38:17.540
and these they still get
from developing countries.

00:38:17.540 --> 00:38:21.350
So that's why I said this fear that robots

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:24.156
will render trade with
developing countries redundant

00:38:24.156 --> 00:38:26.183
has not materialized yet.

00:38:26.183 --> 00:38:29.050
But it's still a fear that
we meet in the horizon

00:38:29.050 --> 00:38:31.420
because so far robots
have not been applied

00:38:31.420 --> 00:38:32.930
in developing countries themselves.

00:38:32.930 --> 00:38:34.490
So of course, the problem becomes

00:38:34.490 --> 00:38:37.240
if in developing countries,
you can use robots

00:38:37.240 --> 00:38:39.340
to produce the parts and the components,

00:38:39.340 --> 00:38:41.440
then eventually jobs in these countries

00:38:41.440 --> 00:38:43.160
are going to be threatened as well.

00:38:43.160 --> 00:38:44.880
So we have not seen that yet

00:38:44.880 --> 00:38:47.023
but it's still a fear that's out there.

00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:53.000
So that creates this
need for the new vision

00:38:54.220 --> 00:38:55.410
that I spoke off.

00:38:55.410 --> 00:38:57.670
So this is something that one

00:38:57.670 --> 00:38:59.660
is immediately confronted with

00:38:59.660 --> 00:39:02.700
if one joins an organization
like the World Bank these days.

00:39:02.700 --> 00:39:04.870
And again to summarize the main point,

00:39:04.870 --> 00:39:08.240
there are two main challenges
that countries face.

00:39:08.240 --> 00:39:10.670
So, developing countries,
poor countries face.

00:39:10.670 --> 00:39:13.258
One is there is this
backlash against free trade

00:39:13.258 --> 00:39:14.800
in developed countries.

00:39:14.800 --> 00:39:17.150
To put it bluntly, many
developed countries

00:39:17.150 --> 00:39:18.620
are closing their borders.

00:39:18.620 --> 00:39:20.060
So, if you are a poor country,

00:39:20.060 --> 00:39:24.490
you cannot really count on
your exports to be welcome

00:39:24.490 --> 00:39:26.610
in richer countries.

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:29.680
And at the same time, there
is a fear of automation

00:39:29.680 --> 00:39:34.040
that may render low wage jobs redundant.

00:39:34.040 --> 00:39:35.960
And on top of that, I
would say there is also

00:39:35.960 --> 00:39:37.790
a lot of competition among
developing countries.

00:39:37.790 --> 00:39:40.972
So if you are in Kenya and you try

00:39:40.972 --> 00:39:45.972
to get into the export market
or into the US export market,

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:48.730
you face competition from Ethiopia,

00:39:48.730 --> 00:39:51.580
you face competition from
Bangladesh, from Vietnam.

00:39:51.580 --> 00:39:54.079
It's a very competitive
space at this point.

00:39:54.079 --> 00:39:56.129
So again, this background,

00:39:56.129 --> 00:39:59.020
these are old model of development.

00:39:59.020 --> 00:40:00.680
So what's the old model?

00:40:00.680 --> 00:40:02.450
Export led industrialization.

00:40:02.450 --> 00:40:04.760
So what seems to have
worked for China or Korea

00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:07.450
or Vietnam is questionable.

00:40:07.450 --> 00:40:10.620
And that raises the question,
what is the alternative?

00:40:10.620 --> 00:40:12.410
So, if this is not going to work,

00:40:12.410 --> 00:40:15.223
what is likely to work for countries?

00:40:16.210 --> 00:40:20.810
So, let me now come into
some of the alternatives

00:40:20.810 --> 00:40:25.400
that have been proposed
in the development world.

00:40:25.400 --> 00:40:28.890
So, the first one is what people call,

00:40:28.890 --> 00:40:30.900
leapfrogging into services.

00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:33.390
The idea there is to leap directly

00:40:33.390 --> 00:40:35.940
from agricultural to the service sector.

00:40:35.940 --> 00:40:39.953
So, bypassing the stage of manufacturing.

00:40:40.960 --> 00:40:45.060
So, the problem with this
is, so far I have not seen

00:40:46.042 --> 00:40:48.380
a legal theoretical
framework and by theoretical,

00:40:48.380 --> 00:40:50.840
I don't necessarily mean many equations

00:40:50.840 --> 00:40:54.290
but some conceptual
framework that clarifies

00:40:54.290 --> 00:40:56.803
how exactly this is supposed to work.

00:40:57.860 --> 00:41:00.300
On the empirical side,
there are many challenges

00:41:00.300 --> 00:41:03.660
if you think about how
this model may work.

00:41:03.660 --> 00:41:06.150
So for one, services is a very broad

00:41:06.150 --> 00:41:07.630
and heterogeneous category.

00:41:07.630 --> 00:41:10.960
So when we talk about services,
we talk about tourists

00:41:10.960 --> 00:41:13.520
but we also talk about
financial and business services.

00:41:13.520 --> 00:41:16.960
You may talk about driving
a car or being a hairdresser

00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:19.560
or you may talk about being
a lawyer or an accountant.

00:41:19.560 --> 00:41:23.090
So it's a very heterogeneous category.

00:41:23.090 --> 00:41:25.790
In addition, so far,
there has been no evidence

00:41:25.790 --> 00:41:28.230
of leapfrogging in any country.

00:41:28.230 --> 00:41:29.740
And again, there, I'm going to rely

00:41:29.740 --> 00:41:33.390
on the World Development Report of 2020.

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:36.050
And that was actually one
of the interesting takeaways

00:41:36.050 --> 00:41:37.240
from this report.

00:41:37.240 --> 00:41:39.640
And let me show you what this graph shows

00:41:39.640 --> 00:41:42.300
because I think it's one of
the most interesting graphs

00:41:42.300 --> 00:41:44.120
in this report.

00:41:44.120 --> 00:41:48.755
So what this graph shows
is how fast countries grow

00:41:48.755 --> 00:41:51.933
once they join,

00:41:55.750 --> 00:41:58.810
once they're set to joint
global value chains.

00:41:58.810 --> 00:42:03.010
And they can join global
value chains in various ways.

00:42:03.010 --> 00:42:06.320
So, you can join by participating

00:42:06.320 --> 00:42:08.590
in what what we call
limited manufacturing.

00:42:08.590 --> 00:42:12.280
So, limited manufacturing
is producing products

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:14.710
that require low skill and low wages.

00:42:14.710 --> 00:42:18.670
So, like textiles, shoes,
the kind of products

00:42:18.670 --> 00:42:23.050
that still Vietnam or Ethiopia produce.

00:42:23.050 --> 00:42:26.520
Then in blue you can have
countries participating

00:42:26.520 --> 00:42:29.847
by joining advanced
manufacturing and services.

00:42:29.847 --> 00:42:33.270
And by advanced
manufacturing, we mean cars,

00:42:33.270 --> 00:42:35.953
so, automobiles, machinery,

00:42:38.281 --> 00:42:42.780
chemicals, so, products
that require more educated,

00:42:42.780 --> 00:42:45.840
a more educated labor
force and more capital.

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:50.130
Then in purple, you have innovative,

00:42:50.130 --> 00:42:52.307
in red, you have innovative activities.

00:42:52.307 --> 00:42:53.703
And these are the kinds of activities

00:42:53.703 --> 00:42:55.450
that the more advanced,

00:42:55.450 --> 00:42:57.473
the richest countries participate in.

00:42:58.659 --> 00:43:03.350
So these include any product that,

00:43:03.350 --> 00:43:07.820
or services that require a
highly skilled labor force.

00:43:07.820 --> 00:43:09.990
And they also include, mostly services.

00:43:09.990 --> 00:43:12.090
So they include mostly things like design

00:43:12.090 --> 00:43:14.410
and research and so on.

00:43:14.410 --> 00:43:19.410
So, you can see, how fast
countries grow once they join

00:43:20.040 --> 00:43:23.010
these global value chains
in one of its forms.

00:43:23.010 --> 00:43:26.230
And so, the fastest growth is seen

00:43:26.230 --> 00:43:30.120
when countries join through
limited manufacturing.

00:43:30.120 --> 00:43:33.931
So that says that from the
time they join year zero,

00:43:33.931 --> 00:43:37.420
20 years later, they have
the per capita income,

00:43:37.420 --> 00:43:41.710
this is all per capita, has
grown cumulatively by 57%.

00:43:41.710 --> 00:43:43.630
So this is a huge number.

00:43:43.630 --> 00:43:46.610
So this is essentially
the story of Vietnam here.

00:43:46.610 --> 00:43:49.800
Then if you are in blue,
it shows what happens

00:43:49.800 --> 00:43:52.330
if you join through advanced manufacturing

00:43:52.330 --> 00:43:53.610
and again, you grow very fast

00:43:53.610 --> 00:43:57.470
but a little, at a lower pace by 48%.

00:43:57.470 --> 00:44:00.880
And in the last case, you
grow by 32% and again,

00:44:00.880 --> 00:44:02.480
this is what you would expect,

00:44:02.480 --> 00:44:05.720
because countries who
join here, the red part,

00:44:05.720 --> 00:44:07.320
these are countries that are already rich.

00:44:07.320 --> 00:44:09.960
So it's harder to actually
become richer and richer,

00:44:09.960 --> 00:44:11.490
it's harder to grow fast.

00:44:11.490 --> 00:44:13.840
So it makes sense that
these numbers are lower.

00:44:14.933 --> 00:44:17.430
And another thing to
point out about this graph

00:44:17.430 --> 00:44:21.550
is countries can be present
in all the three graphs, why?

00:44:21.550 --> 00:44:23.980
Because they may move from one to another.

00:44:23.980 --> 00:44:27.270
So for example, one example is Korea.

00:44:27.270 --> 00:44:30.150
South Korea started here in the green part

00:44:30.150 --> 00:44:32.970
and eventually moved to the blue part

00:44:32.970 --> 00:44:35.160
by participating more
advanced manufacturing,

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:38.080
cars and so on, and by
now it's in the red part.

00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:41.700
Okay, so countries transition
across the three parts.

00:44:41.700 --> 00:44:43.640
So when I show this graph,

00:44:43.640 --> 00:44:46.173
many people ask immediately the question,

00:44:46.173 --> 00:44:48.900
so do we see countries leapfrogging?

00:44:48.900 --> 00:44:52.490
So go directly from being
an agricultural country,

00:44:52.490 --> 00:44:54.110
leapfrogging directly to the red part.

00:44:54.110 --> 00:44:57.680
And this is, if you're in the
World Bank, for many people,

00:44:57.680 --> 00:44:59.070
this is the vision for the future,

00:44:59.070 --> 00:45:01.147
that you are in Africa, you're in Kenya,

00:45:01.147 --> 00:45:04.750
you're in Ethiopia, and
rather than industrializing

00:45:04.750 --> 00:45:06.110
and getting to the green part,

00:45:06.110 --> 00:45:09.010
you are going to jump right
into the red part, right?

00:45:09.010 --> 00:45:12.860
And the fact is that we
don't have a single case,

00:45:12.860 --> 00:45:15.500
a single country where
we've seen that in the past.

00:45:15.500 --> 00:45:17.900
So of course, the fact that
this hasn't happened in the past

00:45:17.900 --> 00:45:19.830
doesn't mean cannot happen in the future.

00:45:19.830 --> 00:45:21.770
But the fact is, we've never seen it.

00:45:21.770 --> 00:45:24.527
So the most successful example is Korea

00:45:24.527 --> 00:45:27.770
and Korea spent again
time in all three parts,

00:45:27.770 --> 00:45:30.040
but it spent very little
time with the green part.

00:45:30.040 --> 00:45:32.530
So very few years in limited manufacturing

00:45:32.530 --> 00:45:35.900
and eventually jumped to
advanced manufacturing.

00:45:35.900 --> 00:45:37.590
But we don't see in the data.

00:45:37.590 --> 00:45:40.070
We've never seen a case
where a country managed

00:45:40.070 --> 00:45:42.405
to go directly from being an agricultural,

00:45:42.405 --> 00:45:47.405
an agrarian setting to going
to being an advanced economy,

00:45:50.810 --> 00:45:53.953
specializing services and
innovative activities.

00:45:53.953 --> 00:45:57.240
So I'm not, again, I don't want to say

00:45:57.240 --> 00:46:00.370
that this cannot happen, but
we don't have any evidence

00:46:00.370 --> 00:46:03.790
and we don't have any theory
to support this so far.

00:46:03.790 --> 00:46:08.790
The second view that you often
hear about is urbanization.

00:46:10.470 --> 00:46:13.763
And again, development has
often, has traditionally been,

00:46:16.414 --> 00:46:18.927
traditionally go hand in
hand with urbanization.

00:46:19.800 --> 00:46:21.820
And we do see increasing urbanization

00:46:21.820 --> 00:46:23.720
in many developing countries.

00:46:23.720 --> 00:46:26.863
Even in Africa, we see the
emergence of big cities.

00:46:27.730 --> 00:46:31.060
However, again, the
evidence here suggests that,

00:46:31.060 --> 00:46:34.530
again, to borrow a term from a paper

00:46:34.530 --> 00:46:37.940
by Gollin, by Dan Gollin who was here

00:46:37.940 --> 00:46:39.543
and Jedwab and Vollrath,

00:46:40.490 --> 00:46:42.500
in many cases, what we see in Africa

00:46:42.500 --> 00:46:45.530
is urbanization without industrialization.

00:46:45.530 --> 00:46:48.860
And when we see that in cities in Africa,

00:46:48.860 --> 00:46:53.100
then we find that the
emergence of the cities

00:46:53.100 --> 00:46:56.140
is not associated with any
good development outcomes.

00:46:56.140 --> 00:46:59.410
So what they show in this
paper by calling at all

00:46:59.410 --> 00:47:03.680
is that these cities, they are large,

00:47:03.680 --> 00:47:06.120
but the poverty rates are still high,

00:47:06.120 --> 00:47:09.170
the educational levels are
high and any measure you use,

00:47:09.170 --> 00:47:14.170
any traditional measure of
development or a wellbeing,

00:47:14.220 --> 00:47:18.330
by any measure the cities
are not doing very well.

00:47:18.330 --> 00:47:20.130
And one point they make in this paper

00:47:20.130 --> 00:47:22.790
is there is a difference between
what they call consumption

00:47:22.790 --> 00:47:23.830
and production cities.

00:47:23.830 --> 00:47:27.790
And consumption cities
are cities associated

00:47:27.790 --> 00:47:28.710
with higher poverty.

00:47:28.710 --> 00:47:30.293
Consumption cities are cities

00:47:30.293 --> 00:47:33.790
where there are a lot of services,

00:47:33.790 --> 00:47:35.007
but these are consumption services.

00:47:35.007 --> 00:47:39.166
So you have many hairdressers
and drivers and the end.

00:47:39.166 --> 00:47:42.061
You have many people
working in these professions

00:47:42.061 --> 00:47:45.560
that are classified as
services but they don't seem

00:47:45.560 --> 00:47:48.410
to be contributing to poverty reduction.

00:47:48.410 --> 00:47:52.060
And this is to be contrasted

00:47:52.060 --> 00:47:53.900
with the so-called production services

00:47:53.900 --> 00:47:57.290
that are associated with trade
that tend to be associated

00:47:57.290 --> 00:47:58.710
with better development outcomes.

00:47:58.710 --> 00:48:01.470
So the point they make in
this paper is that somehow

00:48:01.470 --> 00:48:04.420
in Africa cities cannot play this role

00:48:04.420 --> 00:48:06.953
of being agents of development,

00:48:08.400 --> 00:48:09.770
partly because they concentrate

00:48:09.770 --> 00:48:11.790
on these consumption services.

00:48:11.790 --> 00:48:16.780
So now I want to come
to the third hypothesis,

00:48:16.780 --> 00:48:19.950
the third view that,

00:48:19.950 --> 00:48:22.550
I think deserves further exploration.

00:48:22.550 --> 00:48:25.520
And this relates to the
following intuitive idea

00:48:25.520 --> 00:48:29.320
that if international trade
is not going to be the engine

00:48:29.320 --> 00:48:30.430
of growth in the future,

00:48:30.430 --> 00:48:33.821
simply because of the political reality,

00:48:33.821 --> 00:48:38.090
then perhaps the domestic
market will be asked

00:48:38.090 --> 00:48:40.170
to play a more important role.

00:48:40.170 --> 00:48:43.410
And there I would like to start

00:48:43.410 --> 00:48:45.410
with some casual observation.

00:48:45.410 --> 00:48:49.020
So one observation is that
India has been growing fast.

00:48:49.020 --> 00:48:50.240
We don't know how fast

00:48:50.240 --> 00:48:53.610
because of all the issues with the data.

00:48:53.610 --> 00:48:55.846
No one knows exactly what the numbers are.

00:48:55.846 --> 00:48:57.920
But the one thing that I think

00:48:59.530 --> 00:49:03.380
it's safe to say is that growth
in India has not been driven

00:49:03.380 --> 00:49:04.870
by trade for sure.

00:49:04.870 --> 00:49:07.230
Now, India is a very large country.

00:49:07.230 --> 00:49:09.525
So it's a country where
the large domestic market

00:49:09.525 --> 00:49:13.250
could potentially support growth, why?

00:49:13.250 --> 00:49:15.250
Because a large economy

00:49:15.250 --> 00:49:18.993
can support internal economies of scale.

00:49:20.040 --> 00:49:22.120
A counterexample is Nigeria.

00:49:22.120 --> 00:49:25.460
So Nigeria is also a large
country, not as large as India.

00:49:25.460 --> 00:49:30.360
But it's a large country, is
very rich in natural resources.

00:49:30.360 --> 00:49:33.570
It's reaching people, it
has a large population,

00:49:33.570 --> 00:49:35.860
and it has had periods of fast growth,

00:49:35.860 --> 00:49:38.190
mainly when oil prices go up,

00:49:38.190 --> 00:49:39.940
but there has been no
sustained development

00:49:39.940 --> 00:49:42.050
and no poverty reduction.

00:49:42.050 --> 00:49:45.560
And so, if you contrast these examples,

00:49:45.560 --> 00:49:49.130
one striking difference between the two

00:49:49.130 --> 00:49:52.047
is the degree of inequality

00:49:52.047 --> 00:49:54.870
and this is not to say that
India is an equal economy,

00:49:54.870 --> 00:49:56.040
not at all.

00:49:56.040 --> 00:50:01.040
But Nigeria is well known for
the high degree of inequality,

00:50:02.300 --> 00:50:04.760
so essentially wealth is concentrated

00:50:04.760 --> 00:50:06.580
in the hands of very few.

00:50:06.580 --> 00:50:08.390
And so when there is a positive shock,

00:50:08.390 --> 00:50:11.890
for example, high oil
prices that translate

00:50:11.890 --> 00:50:15.560
to fast aggregate growth, this
growth never trickles down,

00:50:15.560 --> 00:50:18.020
or it trickles down in
the form that Gollin

00:50:18.020 --> 00:50:19.120
and Toill described.

00:50:19.120 --> 00:50:24.120
So you have some reach, you
have a very small reach class.

00:50:24.200 --> 00:50:27.360
They become richer because
oil prices are high.

00:50:27.360 --> 00:50:30.060
They build nice homes, they
need construction workers,

00:50:30.060 --> 00:50:32.560
they need drivers, they need hairdressers,

00:50:32.560 --> 00:50:35.710
they need housekeepers,
these services increase,

00:50:35.710 --> 00:50:37.870
these are consumption services.

00:50:37.870 --> 00:50:39.750
So you have aggregate growth,

00:50:39.750 --> 00:50:42.940
but you don't have sustained
poverty reduction, right?

00:50:42.940 --> 00:50:47.940
So that leads to that to
this additional hypothesis

00:50:48.580 --> 00:50:53.580
that perhaps this high inequality
is actually a constraint

00:50:53.900 --> 00:50:55.783
to growth and poverty reduction.

00:50:56.670 --> 00:51:01.670
And cases like Nigeria or Angola are cases

00:51:02.770 --> 00:51:07.770
where they've exemplified this story.

00:51:08.710 --> 00:51:12.420
So, I try to explain this
story using one or two examples

00:51:12.420 --> 00:51:13.900
but of course, these
are one, two examples.

00:51:13.900 --> 00:51:18.075
And so one question is how do
we operationalize this idea?

00:51:18.075 --> 00:51:21.110
And what exactly does it mean to say,

00:51:21.110 --> 00:51:22.330
we have a large market

00:51:22.330 --> 00:51:24.671
and what's the meaning of
equality in this context?

00:51:24.671 --> 00:51:27.360
How equal is an equal?

00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:29.150
What exactly is development?

00:51:29.150 --> 00:51:31.480
And how large does the market need to be

00:51:31.480 --> 00:51:34.460
in order for these
mechanisms to be relevant?

00:51:34.460 --> 00:51:37.370
And last but not least, so
I gave the example of India

00:51:37.370 --> 00:51:40.710
but if a country is not
India, if a country is small,

00:51:40.710 --> 00:51:42.750
what are you supposed to do?

00:51:42.750 --> 00:51:45.930
This is not going to be, I'm
not going to make the case here

00:51:45.930 --> 00:51:49.360
for countries trying
to expand through war,

00:51:49.360 --> 00:51:52.717
to create Lebensraum, not at all.

00:51:54.234 --> 00:51:57.739
But my point is that one
needs a conceptual framework

00:51:57.739 --> 00:51:59.730
to think about these issues.

00:51:59.730 --> 00:52:02.210
And that actually brings me to some work,

00:52:03.409 --> 00:52:07.530
a paper I've been working on recently.

00:52:07.530 --> 00:52:09.650
And that perhaps is not
a good way to structure

00:52:09.650 --> 00:52:12.980
this talk in the sense
that I'm transitioning

00:52:12.980 --> 00:52:14.720
from either talk like myself

00:52:14.720 --> 00:52:18.403
from what I've done in the World
Bank to more academic work.

00:52:19.480 --> 00:52:22.470
I just want to give you an idea so far

00:52:22.470 --> 00:52:27.470
of what the motivation for
this academic work was.

00:52:27.940 --> 00:52:30.190
So, this is, by the way,
this is a joint project

00:52:30.190 --> 00:52:32.433
with Tristan Reed which here,

00:52:34.300 --> 00:52:37.250
he said the World Bank and we
have a preliminary draft out.

00:52:38.390 --> 00:52:40.600
The idea behind this paper is inspired

00:52:40.600 --> 00:52:44.450
by an paper by Murphy
Shleifer, envisioning from '89

00:52:44.450 --> 00:52:46.680
but that's actually a
paper that makes the case

00:52:46.680 --> 00:52:48.750
for the importance of the domestic market

00:52:48.750 --> 00:52:52.380
a nd a certain degree of equality.

00:52:52.380 --> 00:52:55.190
So this is not their famous
paper on the big push

00:52:55.190 --> 00:52:57.114
in the JP, this is an earlier paper.

00:52:57.114 --> 00:53:00.250
And that paper itself
formalized some of the ideas

00:53:00.250 --> 00:53:02.740
that had been in development
for a very long time before.

00:53:02.740 --> 00:53:05.380
So, most importantly, by Arthur Lewis,

00:53:05.380 --> 00:53:08.267
but also by (mumbles) and other people,

00:53:08.267 --> 00:53:10.880
and I don't want to try
to cover all of them

00:53:10.880 --> 00:53:12.649
because I'm sure I will forget many.

00:53:12.649 --> 00:53:15.880
But the point is that these
ideas were out in development

00:53:15.880 --> 00:53:18.850
for a very long time and the
paper by Murphy and Toill

00:53:18.850 --> 00:53:21.220
actually formalized these ideas.

00:53:21.220 --> 00:53:25.750
And this paper gives rise
to an empirical threshold

00:53:25.750 --> 00:53:30.380
crossing model that we estimate

00:53:30.380 --> 00:53:34.010
using a methodology
very much in the spirit

00:53:34.010 --> 00:53:37.759
of present (mumbles), other
famous paper in the JP,

00:53:37.759 --> 00:53:39.183
the entry paper.

00:53:40.070 --> 00:53:43.870
So we estimate this model using panel data

00:53:43.870 --> 00:53:45.480
from multiple countries.

00:53:45.480 --> 00:53:49.350
And we use it to calculate,
among various other things,

00:53:49.350 --> 00:53:52.420
how large a market needs to be,

00:53:52.420 --> 00:53:55.353
in order to rely on itself,

00:53:55.353 --> 00:53:58.580
to benefit from domestic
economies of scale

00:53:58.580 --> 00:54:01.093
without relying on trade.

00:54:01.970 --> 00:54:06.135
So let me with the remaining
time, which is not much,

00:54:06.135 --> 00:54:09.490
let me outline what this
theoretical framework looks like.

00:54:09.490 --> 00:54:11.770
I'm not going to show any equations here,

00:54:11.770 --> 00:54:13.940
but I'll give you the general idea.

00:54:13.940 --> 00:54:16.120
It's a highly stylized
model, but I just want

00:54:16.120 --> 00:54:17.995
to convey the main idea.

00:54:17.995 --> 00:54:20.167
So, imagine you have a closed economy.

00:54:20.167 --> 00:54:24.030
And this can be relaxed,
but the closed economy case

00:54:24.030 --> 00:54:27.000
is here relevant, because
I want to focus precisely

00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:30.350
on the case where trade
is not important anymore.

00:54:30.350 --> 00:54:31.523
Imagine you have two sectors;

00:54:31.523 --> 00:54:33.853
agricultural and manufacturing.

00:54:34.890 --> 00:54:37.140
What do the preferences look like?

00:54:37.140 --> 00:54:41.233
There is an agricultural good
food and this is a necessity.

00:54:42.430 --> 00:54:45.150
The consumer spend all
their income on food

00:54:45.150 --> 00:54:48.250
until they receive a
minimal subsistence level.

00:54:48.250 --> 00:54:49.890
This is called Z.

00:54:49.890 --> 00:54:52.160
For income levels above Z,

00:54:52.160 --> 00:54:55.513
consumers can afford to buy manufacturers.

00:54:57.530 --> 00:55:00.820
It's assumed that there is a
well defined ordering of goods

00:55:00.820 --> 00:55:02.220
in terms of desirability.

00:55:02.220 --> 00:55:04.610
So, once you are above
the subsistence level,

00:55:04.610 --> 00:55:07.560
you start buying some goods
and then as you become richer

00:55:07.560 --> 00:55:10.730
you buy goods of higher desirability.

00:55:10.730 --> 00:55:14.260
And so as consumption
increases, what in this model,

00:55:14.260 --> 00:55:16.570
this highly stylized model,
what increases is not

00:55:16.570 --> 00:55:18.170
the quantity of the goods you consume,

00:55:18.170 --> 00:55:21.340
what increases is the
variety of goods you consume,

00:55:21.340 --> 00:55:23.400
the range of goods.

00:55:23.400 --> 00:55:25.480
In terms of technology,
there are two inputs;

00:55:25.480 --> 00:55:27.180
land and labor.

00:55:27.180 --> 00:55:28.450
Land is a fixed factor.

00:55:28.450 --> 00:55:31.250
So, as a result, there
are decreasing returns

00:55:31.250 --> 00:55:33.080
to scale in agriculture.

00:55:33.080 --> 00:55:34.430
And then in manufacturing
that's important,

00:55:34.430 --> 00:55:35.780
there are two technologies.

00:55:35.780 --> 00:55:38.660
There is a constant
returns to scale technology

00:55:39.703 --> 00:55:41.390
that has no fixed costs.

00:55:41.390 --> 00:55:44.813
And there is also an increasing
returns to scale technology

00:55:44.813 --> 00:55:47.753
that has fixed costs but
lower variable costs.

00:55:48.620 --> 00:55:50.550
So, firms have a choice, they can,

00:55:50.550 --> 00:55:53.080
if they want to produce manufactured goods

00:55:53.080 --> 00:55:56.120
they can produce using
the constant returns

00:55:56.120 --> 00:55:59.420
to scale technology, or
they can use the IRS,

00:55:59.420 --> 00:56:01.460
increasing return to scale technology.

00:56:01.460 --> 00:56:04.860
And industrialization
in this setup is defined

00:56:04.860 --> 00:56:07.810
as the transition from the
constant returns to scale

00:56:07.810 --> 00:56:09.363
to the IRS technology.

00:56:10.760 --> 00:56:13.500
The market structure is
as follows in agriculture,

00:56:13.500 --> 00:56:16.190
you have perfect competition
in manufacturing.

00:56:16.190 --> 00:56:18.250
If you produce with a
back stop technology,

00:56:18.250 --> 00:56:19.860
there is perfect competition.

00:56:19.860 --> 00:56:21.320
But if you are in manufacturing,

00:56:21.320 --> 00:56:23.430
and you produce with the IRS technology,

00:56:23.430 --> 00:56:26.500
so if you are industrialized,
then there are monopoly,

00:56:26.500 --> 00:56:28.210
there is a monopoly, the monopoly rents

00:56:28.210 --> 00:56:29.700
and the monopoly rents actually play

00:56:29.700 --> 00:56:32.370
an important role in this model.

00:56:32.370 --> 00:56:33.720
And finally, that's important,

00:56:33.720 --> 00:56:35.829
that that's the appealing
feature of this model.

00:56:35.829 --> 00:56:38.290
It's assumed that there
is poverty and inequality

00:56:38.290 --> 00:56:39.530
to start with.

00:56:39.530 --> 00:56:43.200
Poverty here is defined
as follows, the wage,

00:56:43.200 --> 00:56:45.210
there is only one wage in the economy.

00:56:45.210 --> 00:56:48.370
The wage in agriculture is
the same as in manufacturing.

00:56:48.370 --> 00:56:51.570
And this wage is below
the subsistence level.

00:56:51.570 --> 00:56:55.150
So if all you do is being
a worker in agriculture,

00:56:55.150 --> 00:56:57.830
then you can't buy
manufacturers and you're poor.

00:56:57.830 --> 00:56:59.520
You don't have enough to eat.

00:56:59.520 --> 00:57:01.750
On the other hand and there's also

00:57:01.750 --> 00:57:04.400
a very high degree of
inequality in the sense

00:57:04.400 --> 00:57:07.010
that most people are laborers.

00:57:07.010 --> 00:57:10.590
So they rely only on the labor
income, and they're poor.

00:57:10.590 --> 00:57:13.270
However, there is also a
fraction in this population

00:57:13.270 --> 00:57:17.493
that owns land rents and shares.

00:57:18.530 --> 00:57:21.520
And so this is the structure.

00:57:21.520 --> 00:57:25.960
And then what they show is
that with these assumptions,

00:57:25.960 --> 00:57:27.270
you can have an equilibrium,

00:57:27.270 --> 00:57:30.647
it's actually a unique equilibrium
with industrialization.

00:57:30.647 --> 00:57:34.950
And in this equilibrium,
you have the monopolies,

00:57:34.950 --> 00:57:38.730
they show that the monopolist
will adopt the IRS technology,

00:57:38.730 --> 00:57:43.440
when the variable costs, when
the profits are positive.

00:57:43.440 --> 00:57:46.260
And that means that the
variable profits are high enough

00:57:46.260 --> 00:57:48.430
to cover the fixed costs.

00:57:48.430 --> 00:57:51.690
So in the margin sector of the
economy, the variable profits

00:57:51.690 --> 00:57:53.960
are going to be exactly
equal to the fixed costs.

00:57:53.960 --> 00:57:55.838
So this is exactly what gives rise

00:57:55.838 --> 00:57:58.520
to this breakeven condition

00:57:58.520 --> 00:58:00.620
and for those of you
familiar with the model,

00:58:00.620 --> 00:58:04.210
you see now why I said
this is very similar

00:58:04.210 --> 00:58:06.630
to the setup in Bresnahan and Reiss.

00:58:06.630 --> 00:58:09.620
In Bresnahan and Reiss
they estimate thresholds,

00:58:09.620 --> 00:58:14.410
market threshold sizes
for a duopoly to enter,

00:58:14.410 --> 00:58:17.280
for more firms to enter market.

00:58:17.280 --> 00:58:19.760
Here, we don't do that but here
we simply asked the question

00:58:19.760 --> 00:58:23.560
how large does a market need
to be for the monopolist

00:58:23.560 --> 00:58:28.560
to view this IRS technology as desirable.

00:58:28.560 --> 00:58:31.433
So to switch from the CRS
to the IRS technology.

00:58:32.350 --> 00:58:36.950
Now, variable profits
themselves depend on sales.

00:58:36.950 --> 00:58:40.580
So sales have to be large
enough to cover the fixed costs.

00:58:40.580 --> 00:58:44.600
So denote by N star, the
minimum efficient scale.

00:58:44.600 --> 00:58:46.590
Then the sales are going
to depend on two things

00:58:46.590 --> 00:58:47.550
in this setup.

00:58:47.550 --> 00:58:50.650
They're going to depend on the
size of the domestic market.

00:58:50.650 --> 00:58:53.247
And so that's why a large
domestic market is going to help

00:58:53.247 --> 00:58:57.140
but they also depend on the
size of the middle class.

00:58:57.140 --> 00:58:59.870
So now introduce another
term here, middle class,

00:58:59.870 --> 00:59:01.950
what's the middle class in this model?

00:59:01.950 --> 00:59:04.069
So middle class is the number of consumers

00:59:04.069 --> 00:59:05.950
who are not very poor.

00:59:05.950 --> 00:59:10.571
So they have enough money
to buy manufacturers

00:59:10.571 --> 00:59:14.210
but they are not so
rich that they buy only

00:59:14.210 --> 00:59:16.960
a few luxury goods that
you can actually produce

00:59:16.960 --> 00:59:19.640
with this backstop technology.

00:59:19.640 --> 00:59:21.970
So they're actually the
consumers that have incomes

00:59:21.970 --> 00:59:23.500
between the subsistence level

00:59:23.500 --> 00:59:26.370
and between the minimum
efficient scale, okay?

00:59:26.370 --> 00:59:29.220
So in other words, what
happens in this economy

00:59:29.220 --> 00:59:33.130
is if you are really poor,
if you rely only on your wage

00:59:33.130 --> 00:59:34.970
that's below the subsistence level,

00:59:34.970 --> 00:59:36.872
then you just buy food, nothing else.

00:59:36.872 --> 00:59:41.872
If you have some shares
in land or in industry,

00:59:45.860 --> 00:59:49.390
within this realization, then you can buy,

00:59:49.390 --> 00:59:53.070
you use your first Z units
of your income to buy food.

00:59:53.070 --> 00:59:54.770
Once you've reached the subsistence level,

00:59:54.770 --> 00:59:58.250
you use the next income to
buy industrialized goods.

00:59:58.250 --> 01:00:02.420
So goods that are produced at mass scale.

01:00:02.420 --> 01:00:04.760
And then if you have even more money,

01:00:04.760 --> 01:00:07.350
if you're in the upper
class, if you're truly rich,

01:00:07.350 --> 01:00:09.513
that's the upper class, then you buy food,

01:00:09.513 --> 01:00:12.920
you buy these goods
that are mass produced,

01:00:12.920 --> 01:00:15.490
the goods that are also
bought by the middle class.

01:00:15.490 --> 01:00:18.635
But you also buy the backstop,
you also buy the luxury goods

01:00:18.635 --> 01:00:20.800
that are produced with a constant returns

01:00:20.800 --> 01:00:22.139
to scale technology.

01:00:22.139 --> 01:00:24.530
So I think this model has, in my view,

01:00:24.530 --> 01:00:26.300
it's very simple, very stylized,

01:00:26.300 --> 01:00:28.540
but it has a number of
very attractive features,

01:00:28.540 --> 01:00:30.290
because you can use it to explain

01:00:30.290 --> 01:00:31.930
what's happening in many
developing countries.

01:00:31.930 --> 01:00:33.610
So if you think about Nigeria,

01:00:33.610 --> 01:00:35.390
the example I gave you before,

01:00:35.390 --> 01:00:37.040
so this is a case where the middle class

01:00:37.040 --> 01:00:38.510
is relatively small.

01:00:38.510 --> 01:00:43.030
So you have resources,
but you have the very poor

01:00:43.030 --> 01:00:45.550
buying just food and then
you have the very rich

01:00:45.550 --> 01:00:46.970
buying just luxury goods.

01:00:46.970 --> 01:00:50.220
So goods that you don't produce,
they're not mass produced

01:00:50.220 --> 01:00:52.990
with IRS technology.

01:00:52.990 --> 01:00:54.880
And, in these cases,

01:00:54.880 --> 01:00:57.740
you end up with these
consumption services and growth

01:00:57.740 --> 01:00:59.350
that doesn't trickle down.

01:00:59.350 --> 01:01:01.920
So I think it very much,
you can use that to explain

01:01:01.920 --> 01:01:04.010
what happens in many developing countries.

01:01:04.010 --> 01:01:06.200
Now, what are the
implications for development?

01:01:06.200 --> 01:01:11.200
You can apply this model

01:01:11.430 --> 01:01:16.000
to tell a story about
structural transformation

01:01:16.000 --> 01:01:17.370
that looks as follows.

01:01:17.370 --> 01:01:20.980
So a poor country, take
again the example I gave you,

01:01:20.980 --> 01:01:23.400
Nigeria, needs an initial boost.

01:01:23.400 --> 01:01:24.780
So you need something that is going

01:01:24.780 --> 01:01:27.310
to create resources wealth effect.

01:01:27.310 --> 01:01:29.990
And this can be an increase
in agricultural productivity.

01:01:29.990 --> 01:01:30.960
This is again something

01:01:30.960 --> 01:01:33.780
that the early development
literature often emphasize

01:01:33.780 --> 01:01:37.060
that you need agricultural
productivity to increase,

01:01:37.060 --> 01:01:39.473
otherwise, you don't have,

01:01:41.180 --> 01:01:44.950
you cannot have enough resources free

01:01:44.950 --> 01:01:48.260
from agriculture to go to manufacturing.

01:01:48.260 --> 01:01:51.550
But alternatively, you
can also have exports

01:01:51.550 --> 01:01:52.680
of natural resources.

01:01:52.680 --> 01:01:55.660
For example, in the case of
Nigeria, you can have oil.

01:01:55.660 --> 01:01:58.170
And that's where the model
becomes a little more,

01:01:58.170 --> 01:02:00.750
allows for a little openness in the sense

01:02:00.750 --> 01:02:03.375
that in order to export
oil you need to be,

01:02:03.375 --> 01:02:05.890
you need to be trading
with another country.

01:02:05.890 --> 01:02:09.670
But here trade in that case
has a very specific function.

01:02:09.670 --> 01:02:14.640
It's not a case where you use trade

01:02:14.640 --> 01:02:18.003
to tap into foreign markets
and exploit economies of scale,

01:02:18.003 --> 01:02:21.710
you just use trade to generate
this initial wealth effect.

01:02:21.710 --> 01:02:23.247
To just bring revenue into the country

01:02:23.247 --> 01:02:25.070
and this is the only function

01:02:25.070 --> 01:02:27.310
that these oil revenues have, okay?

01:02:27.310 --> 01:02:30.250
So, you have this wealth
effect, and this wealth effect

01:02:30.250 --> 01:02:33.920
can jumpstart the process of
structural transformation, how?

01:02:33.920 --> 01:02:38.240
So, take the case of agricultural
productivity increasing,

01:02:38.240 --> 01:02:42.010
then the wages rise both
because productivity increases,

01:02:42.010 --> 01:02:46.984
and because there is more demand
for labor in agricultural.

01:02:46.984 --> 01:02:49.900
Then the rents rise and labor move

01:02:49.900 --> 01:02:51.930
from agriculture to manufacturing.

01:02:51.930 --> 01:02:55.020
As manufacturing expands,
agricultural shrinks.

01:02:55.020 --> 01:02:58.140
So this is the process
of industrialization.

01:02:58.140 --> 01:03:03.140
And so the main insights of
this model is that what you need

01:03:03.514 --> 01:03:06.840
for industrialization
to happen is you need

01:03:06.840 --> 01:03:07.990
this initial boost.

01:03:07.990 --> 01:03:10.330
So, this can be as I said
agricultural productivity

01:03:10.330 --> 01:03:12.091
or initial growth in exports.

01:03:12.091 --> 01:03:14.810
You need to have a large domestic market

01:03:14.810 --> 01:03:17.300
and you also need a
certain degree of equality,

01:03:17.300 --> 01:03:20.820
and here equality is interpreted
in a very specific way.

01:03:20.820 --> 01:03:24.150
What you need is the middle class,

01:03:24.150 --> 01:03:28.190
you need people with
enough purchasing power

01:03:28.190 --> 01:03:32.130
that are going to support
producing products

01:03:33.470 --> 01:03:36.345
at mass scale and taking advantage

01:03:36.345 --> 01:03:41.345
of decreasing returns to scale.

01:03:41.660 --> 01:03:43.950
Okay, so one question you may have is, so,

01:03:43.950 --> 01:03:47.520
here industrialization and
development are synonyms

01:03:47.520 --> 01:03:49.950
in this model, and one
question is why does this lead

01:03:49.950 --> 01:03:53.260
to poverty reduction, which
is what I'm interested in.

01:03:53.260 --> 01:03:55.150
So directly there is an effect

01:03:55.150 --> 01:03:57.020
because the more the wages increase

01:03:57.020 --> 01:04:00.170
as wages increase, eventually,
the living standards

01:04:00.170 --> 01:04:05.040
of the poor improve and
they may rise above poverty.

01:04:05.040 --> 01:04:07.070
In addition profits increase.

01:04:07.070 --> 01:04:10.350
And so, the living standards
of the small shareholders

01:04:10.350 --> 01:04:12.330
to the extent of the small shareholders

01:04:12.330 --> 01:04:15.110
may be below the poverty
line also improve.

01:04:15.110 --> 01:04:17.940
But there are also reasons
that are outside the model.

01:04:17.940 --> 01:04:20.950
So, for example, there may
be technological spillovers

01:04:20.950 --> 01:04:23.490
associated with industrialization.

01:04:23.490 --> 01:04:26.320
So, these are not in the
model, but in other literature,

01:04:26.320 --> 01:04:29.540
they've been shown to be
very important for growth.

01:04:29.540 --> 01:04:32.090
And empirically,
industrialization has always

01:04:32.090 --> 01:04:33.710
been associated with development.

01:04:33.710 --> 01:04:38.710
So, you can rely on this
empirical observation.

01:04:39.028 --> 01:04:42.773
So, this is sort of the theoretical
background of the model.

01:04:43.680 --> 01:04:48.680
So this theory structure leads
to threshold crossing model

01:04:49.720 --> 01:04:51.150
that looks as follows.

01:04:51.150 --> 01:04:53.140
That's again very simple.

01:04:53.140 --> 01:04:57.860
The profit, the increasing
return to sector

01:04:57.860 --> 01:04:59.477
are given as follows.

01:04:59.477 --> 01:05:03.670
The market size times per
capita variable profit.

01:05:03.670 --> 01:05:07.063
So these are the variable
profits minus fixed costs.

01:05:08.300 --> 01:05:10.470
So follow again, that's
the part that's inspired

01:05:10.470 --> 01:05:12.043
by Bresnahan and Reiss.

01:05:12.980 --> 01:05:14.690
The beauty of Bresnahan and Reiss

01:05:14.690 --> 01:05:16.769
is they could estimate an entry model

01:05:16.769 --> 01:05:21.330
without using data on prices,
which are very hard to get.

01:05:21.330 --> 01:05:23.211
Of course, I know Steve is going to say

01:05:23.211 --> 01:05:27.837
this is a huge weakness of
this literature and I agree,

01:05:27.837 --> 01:05:30.650
but if you are trying to
think about poverty here

01:05:30.650 --> 01:05:34.110
for 100 countries, this
becomes an advantage

01:05:34.110 --> 01:05:37.450
because you can try to
relay all these entities

01:05:37.450 --> 01:05:40.690
I talked about, variable
profits or fixed costs

01:05:40.690 --> 01:05:45.370
to market observables rather than prices.

01:05:45.370 --> 01:05:50.370
So we model here market size
as a function of observables.

01:05:50.530 --> 01:05:54.230
And similarly, variable profit
as a function of demand,

01:05:54.230 --> 01:05:57.970
observables and cost, demand
variables and cost variables

01:05:57.970 --> 01:06:00.360
and same thing with fixed costs.

01:06:00.360 --> 01:06:04.203
And a firm is going to
enter the IRS sector.

01:06:06.630 --> 01:06:09.970
So it's going to adopt
this new technology,

01:06:09.970 --> 01:06:13.660
if the variable profits
exceed the fixed costs,

01:06:13.660 --> 01:06:18.660
and so that gives rise
to this empirical model.

01:06:19.810 --> 01:06:23.771
So, let me now come to the part

01:06:23.771 --> 01:06:26.760
that makes a direct
connection to the empirics.

01:06:26.760 --> 01:06:29.930
As I said, we model all these things,

01:06:29.930 --> 01:06:33.406
variable profits or fixed costs
as functions of observables.

01:06:33.406 --> 01:06:35.650
And these observables are as follows.

01:06:35.650 --> 01:06:39.377
So, in terms of market size,
so we define market size

01:06:39.377 --> 01:06:41.840
as a function of the following variables.

01:06:41.840 --> 01:06:45.350
So, the population of the
country in terms of raw bodies

01:06:45.350 --> 01:06:48.160
and in particular in this specification

01:06:48.160 --> 01:06:50.523
is how many people are,

01:06:51.520 --> 01:06:53.470
people who don't have the purchasing power

01:06:53.470 --> 01:06:55.000
of the middle class.

01:06:55.000 --> 01:06:58.230
Then the market size
also depends on the share

01:06:58.230 --> 01:07:01.220
of the middle class and I
will tell you in a moment

01:07:01.220 --> 01:07:02.904
how we define that.

01:07:02.904 --> 01:07:06.320
And in addition, we take
into account the fact

01:07:06.320 --> 01:07:08.380
that if you actually take
this model to the data,

01:07:08.380 --> 01:07:10.570
in reality, most countries
are not closed, right?

01:07:10.570 --> 01:07:13.900
In the model I presented, we
started with a stylized case

01:07:13.900 --> 01:07:15.760
of a closed economy, but actually,

01:07:15.760 --> 01:07:18.230
most countries are open to trade.

01:07:18.230 --> 01:07:20.960
So then to take that into account,

01:07:20.960 --> 01:07:24.750
we define two measures of integration.

01:07:24.750 --> 01:07:29.750
One is based on summing up the
populations of all countries

01:07:30.005 --> 01:07:32.930
with which a particular
countries integrated with

01:07:32.930 --> 01:07:34.910
and the other one is based on the,

01:07:34.910 --> 01:07:38.000
on summing up all the
incomes of the countries

01:07:38.000 --> 01:07:42.260
with which a country is integrated with.

01:07:42.260 --> 01:07:45.392
So that's actually I think, one
of the biggest contributions

01:07:45.392 --> 01:07:48.584
of this work, the way
we define integration,

01:07:48.584 --> 01:07:52.110
so in order to see which
countries are integrated

01:07:52.110 --> 01:07:57.110
with others, we exploit
information on participation

01:07:58.830 --> 01:08:00.650
in the World Trade Organization.

01:08:00.650 --> 01:08:03.280
GAD but also many
regional trade agreements

01:08:03.280 --> 01:08:07.130
and we relied on some
work by other authors

01:08:07.130 --> 01:08:10.784
that actually went down all
the regional trade agreements

01:08:10.784 --> 01:08:14.890
and go down the legal
provisions in each case,

01:08:14.890 --> 01:08:16.890
which case there were legal provisions

01:08:16.890 --> 01:08:19.710
that connected countries.

01:08:19.710 --> 01:08:24.710
So, if there is a legal provision
connecting two countries,

01:08:25.260 --> 01:08:29.790
then we assign an indicator equal to one

01:08:29.790 --> 01:08:32.400
and then we use that to form weights

01:08:32.400 --> 01:08:34.760
summing up all the countries.

01:08:34.760 --> 01:08:38.054
So the main, I will try to
avoid getting into the details

01:08:38.054 --> 01:08:41.370
given time constraints, but the main point

01:08:41.370 --> 01:08:43.250
I want to make here is that we end up

01:08:43.250 --> 01:08:45.950
using two different measures
of international integration.

01:08:45.950 --> 01:08:48.570
And one base is based on the idea

01:08:48.570 --> 01:08:50.230
that when you connect
with other countries,

01:08:50.230 --> 01:08:52.930
you may connect with other countries

01:08:52.930 --> 01:08:54.510
that are very large in population.

01:08:54.510 --> 01:08:58.160
So this way you get access
to a lot of consumers,

01:08:58.160 --> 01:08:59.750
but there is an also
an alternative measure

01:08:59.750 --> 01:09:02.380
that rely to the notion that
you connect with countries

01:09:02.380 --> 01:09:05.250
that may be very rich,
they may have high income.

01:09:05.250 --> 01:09:06.530
And this is also viable,

01:09:06.530 --> 01:09:09.140
because you may connect to countries

01:09:09.140 --> 01:09:11.696
that are small in terms of
population, but they're very,

01:09:11.696 --> 01:09:13.823
that they have large purchasing power.

01:09:14.890 --> 01:09:17.920
So in terms of the per
capita variable profits,

01:09:17.920 --> 01:09:21.110
there again, we, the choice of variables

01:09:21.110 --> 01:09:23.380
is very much informed by
the model in the sense

01:09:23.380 --> 01:09:25.920
that we use past export growth

01:09:25.920 --> 01:09:28.920
and also past agricultural
productivity growth

01:09:28.920 --> 01:09:33.690
as observables that may lead
to higher variable profits.

01:09:33.690 --> 01:09:36.350
And finally, when it
comes to the fixed costs,

01:09:36.350 --> 01:09:39.690
that's when we rely on
the very large literature

01:09:40.760 --> 01:09:45.300
on institutions in developing countries,

01:09:45.300 --> 01:09:49.113
which suggests that variables
such as the tropical climate,

01:09:50.736 --> 01:09:54.600
the desert climate, distance
to the coast, ruggedness

01:09:54.600 --> 01:09:57.050
and also legal origins,

01:09:57.050 --> 01:09:59.550
whether they're British legal origins

01:09:59.550 --> 01:10:02.790
or French Typical origins have an impact

01:10:02.790 --> 01:10:07.790
to how well a country is run,
which in turn may have effect

01:10:08.160 --> 01:10:10.020
on the size of the fixed costs.

01:10:10.020 --> 01:10:13.550
So these are the observables
we use and again there,

01:10:13.550 --> 01:10:15.320
you can think of many robustness checks

01:10:15.320 --> 01:10:17.053
and many alternative definitions.

01:10:18.530 --> 01:10:23.050
The dependent variable we use in this work

01:10:23.050 --> 01:10:28.050
is sustained poverty reduction.

01:10:29.200 --> 01:10:32.650
And so, this is, so, in other words,

01:10:32.650 --> 01:10:35.910
the question we are asking is,

01:10:35.910 --> 01:10:40.550
can we we relay sustained
poverty reduction

01:10:40.550 --> 01:10:42.640
to industrialization?

01:10:42.640 --> 01:10:46.210
And if so, how large
does a market need to be

01:10:46.210 --> 01:10:48.370
for this process to be relevant?

01:10:48.370 --> 01:10:50.810
So, in order to do that we need

01:10:50.810 --> 01:10:52.080
to find we need to define

01:10:52.080 --> 01:10:54.900
what sustained poverty reduction is.

01:10:54.900 --> 01:10:57.220
And without again, going into the details,

01:10:57.220 --> 01:10:59.510
I'll just give you a brief overview.

01:10:59.510 --> 01:11:01.400
We use the POVCALNET.

01:11:01.400 --> 01:11:05.190
This is a computational tool
developed by the World Bank

01:11:05.190 --> 01:11:08.760
that allows you to compute poverty rates

01:11:08.760 --> 01:11:10.280
for many different countries.

01:11:10.280 --> 01:11:15.070
We use that to compute poverty
rates for particular years.

01:11:15.070 --> 01:11:17.660
And then in some cases,
we have to interpolate

01:11:17.660 --> 01:11:19.170
given missing data.

01:11:19.170 --> 01:11:24.170
And then we break up our
data into five-year periods.

01:11:24.440 --> 01:11:29.440
And then we look, we
look at whether poverty

01:11:29.520 --> 01:11:33.640
has declined in every single
year in this five-year period.

01:11:33.640 --> 01:11:36.920
So if poverty has declined
every year in five-year period,

01:11:36.920 --> 01:11:40.723
then for this period, we
assign a variable equal to one,

01:11:41.850 --> 01:11:43.230
saying that during that period,

01:11:43.230 --> 01:11:45.720
we had sustained poverty reduction, right?

01:11:45.720 --> 01:11:47.960
If poverty declines in the first two years

01:11:47.960 --> 01:11:52.220
and then goes up in the
next year, then we assign

01:11:52.220 --> 01:11:54.950
a value equal to zero to this measure

01:11:54.950 --> 01:11:56.720
of sustained poverty reduction.

01:11:56.720 --> 01:11:59.600
So in other words, what
we try to do and again,

01:11:59.600 --> 01:12:01.050
there are many different ways to do that,

01:12:01.050 --> 01:12:03.430
but one has to start somewhere is to say,

01:12:03.430 --> 01:12:07.350
do we see countries reducing poverty

01:12:08.650 --> 01:12:10.767
over a period of time every single year?

01:12:10.767 --> 01:12:14.620
If so, then we call that
sustained poverty reduction.

01:12:14.620 --> 01:12:18.011
Okay, so this is going to
be our dependent variable.

01:12:18.011 --> 01:12:21.250
So let me show you, some
patterns in the data

01:12:21.250 --> 01:12:22.990
that I think quite interesting.

01:12:22.990 --> 01:12:25.260
So we have a total of 93 countries.

01:12:25.260 --> 01:12:28.990
We exclude countries that have
already eliminated poverty.

01:12:28.990 --> 01:12:30.770
So these are the advanced countries

01:12:30.770 --> 01:12:34.636
or all countries that have
poverty rates below 3%.

01:12:34.636 --> 01:12:37.390
And again poverty is defined
in terms of extreme poverty,

01:12:37.390 --> 01:12:41.150
so being below the 1.9 threshold.

01:12:41.150 --> 01:12:44.820
And what this table shows you

01:12:44.820 --> 01:12:48.810
is how sustained real
per capita GDP growth

01:12:48.810 --> 01:12:51.410
relates to sustained
extreme poverty reduction.

01:12:51.410 --> 01:12:54.240
So what we did for poverty,
you can also do for growth,

01:12:54.240 --> 01:12:56.440
for per capita growth, income growth.

01:12:56.440 --> 01:12:57.870
You can take per capita income growth

01:12:57.870 --> 01:12:58.863
and see if it,

01:13:02.177 --> 01:13:04.004
if there is growth every single period,

01:13:04.004 --> 01:13:08.220
then you would call it sustained
per capita income growth.

01:13:08.220 --> 01:13:12.230
So, as you would expect,
growth and poverty reduction

01:13:12.230 --> 01:13:14.520
are highly correlated, and this is a point

01:13:14.520 --> 01:13:16.720
that people have often made,

01:13:16.720 --> 01:13:20.040
growth is important for
poverty, this is undeniable.

01:13:20.040 --> 01:13:25.040
So, 76%, if we see poverty
reduction in 76% of the cases,

01:13:26.480 --> 01:13:29.590
this is also accompanied by growth.

01:13:29.590 --> 01:13:33.710
However, there are also many
cases, so 82 in particular

01:13:33.710 --> 01:13:36.800
out of the 347, where you do have growth

01:13:36.800 --> 01:13:38.970
but you don't see poverty reduction.

01:13:38.970 --> 01:13:43.030
So growth doesn't automatically
generate poverty reduction.

01:13:43.030 --> 01:13:45.890
And we see these, these
are all cases in countries

01:13:45.890 --> 01:13:47.150
that are extremely unequal.

01:13:47.150 --> 01:13:49.230
So, again Nigeria comes to mind .

01:13:49.230 --> 01:13:52.450
These are cases where you
make up very fast growth

01:13:52.450 --> 01:13:55.930
usually fueled by commodity prices,

01:13:58.470 --> 01:14:00.963
but then this growth never trickles down.

01:14:02.420 --> 01:14:04.806
So this is the first thing to show.

01:14:04.806 --> 01:14:07.170
The second is again showing the data

01:14:07.170 --> 01:14:10.230
on sustained extreme poverty reduction.

01:14:10.230 --> 01:14:15.230
So here, what we graphed is how many cases

01:14:16.800 --> 01:14:20.570
of sustained poverty reduction
do you have over time

01:14:20.570 --> 01:14:22.570
in various parts of the world.

01:14:22.570 --> 01:14:25.000
And the first thing that jumps
out if you look at this graph

01:14:25.000 --> 01:14:28.710
is how high the percentages associated

01:14:28.710 --> 01:14:31.120
with sustained poverty reduction are.

01:14:31.120 --> 01:14:33.870
So most of the numbers are well above 50%.

01:14:33.870 --> 01:14:38.870
So here, for many countries
in 86% of these country

01:14:40.210 --> 01:14:43.830
five-year pairs, you see
sustained poverty reduction.

01:14:43.830 --> 01:14:45.200
And again, this speaks to the fact

01:14:45.200 --> 01:14:47.220
that there has been enormous progress made

01:14:47.220 --> 01:14:50.360
so we do see poverty reduction over time.

01:14:50.360 --> 01:14:52.990
Our data, by the way,
are from 1980 to 2015.

01:14:54.820 --> 01:14:56.869
There are some other things

01:14:56.869 --> 01:14:57.702
that I think are quite interesting.

01:14:57.702 --> 01:15:00.480
If you look at the period 2006 to 2010,

01:15:00.480 --> 01:15:03.406
so this is post-financial crisis.

01:15:03.406 --> 01:15:07.260
This is still a period where
poverty goes down very fast

01:15:07.260 --> 01:15:08.830
in many parts of the world.

01:15:08.830 --> 01:15:12.770
And that again shows
that advanced countries,

01:15:12.770 --> 01:15:15.440
developing countries are
not always synchronized.

01:15:15.440 --> 01:15:17.690
So even though the advanced countries,

01:15:17.690 --> 01:15:20.480
the industrialized world
was in a major crisis,

01:15:20.480 --> 01:15:23.840
there were still progress made
in many developing countries.

01:15:23.840 --> 01:15:26.870
Some cases that really
stand out is Africa.

01:15:26.870 --> 01:15:29.500
Again, Sub Saharan Africa until '96,

01:15:29.500 --> 01:15:32.170
you have hardly any poverty reduction.

01:15:32.170 --> 01:15:34.400
And again, this is well known.

01:15:34.400 --> 01:15:37.483
In other cases, Latin America,
which is more puzzling.

01:15:38.450 --> 01:15:40.800
And we think that has
more to do with the data.

01:15:41.800 --> 01:15:43.250
Let me also show you some patterns

01:15:43.250 --> 01:15:44.663
on international integration

01:15:44.663 --> 01:15:47.120
that I think are quite interesting.

01:15:47.120 --> 01:15:49.170
As I mentioned, there are two measures

01:15:49.170 --> 01:15:50.530
of international integration.

01:15:50.530 --> 01:15:54.070
One captures the size of
the international market,

01:15:54.070 --> 01:15:57.250
the integrated market as
measured by population,

01:15:57.250 --> 01:15:59.763
and the other one as measured by income.

01:16:01.450 --> 01:16:02.640
By relative income.

01:16:02.640 --> 01:16:06.450
And so what we do here is
actually we construct a variable

01:16:06.450 --> 01:16:09.840
that measures integration
in relative terms.

01:16:09.840 --> 01:16:14.090
So what's the size of the
market in terms of population

01:16:14.090 --> 01:16:16.830
that you connect with
relative to your own market?

01:16:16.830 --> 01:16:18.520
These are all in relative terms.

01:16:18.520 --> 01:16:22.500
So one thing you see is
starting in 2000, around 2000,

01:16:22.500 --> 01:16:24.964
in almost the entire world,

01:16:24.964 --> 01:16:29.140
the integrated market jumps up, right?

01:16:29.140 --> 01:16:33.640
So in terms of population,
countries are connecting

01:16:33.640 --> 01:16:35.250
to much larger market.

01:16:35.250 --> 01:16:38.670
So what's going on here is very,
very simply, this is China,

01:16:38.670 --> 01:16:41.840
You connect to China, you
connect to a very large market,

01:16:41.840 --> 01:16:44.390
but at the same time, see what happens.

01:16:44.390 --> 01:16:48.140
If you define this integration
measure in terms of income,

01:16:48.140 --> 01:16:49.920
then it goes down.

01:16:49.920 --> 01:16:54.800
And again, that's a
graphical way of portraying

01:16:54.800 --> 01:16:56.360
the entry of China.

01:16:56.360 --> 01:16:59.120
This is another way to
see that very clearly.

01:16:59.120 --> 01:17:02.410
It's again, 2001, this
is just for countries

01:17:02.410 --> 01:17:04.920
that are members of the
World Trade Organization.

01:17:04.920 --> 01:17:09.920
So in 2001, in terms of
population, there is a jump.

01:17:10.770 --> 01:17:12.770
It seems that countries are connecting

01:17:12.770 --> 01:17:16.900
the relative integrated
market in terms of population

01:17:16.900 --> 01:17:19.010
becomes much larger than before

01:17:19.010 --> 01:17:22.010
because China enters the
World Trade Organization.

01:17:22.010 --> 01:17:24.776
But in terms of income,
you have this huge jump,

01:17:24.776 --> 01:17:27.007
but then the income in China rises.

01:17:27.007 --> 01:17:29.970
And so then again, the
relative income rises.

01:17:29.970 --> 01:17:34.140
So this is one way of
portraying what has happened,

01:17:34.140 --> 01:17:37.240
but at the same time, you can
also see what the concern is.

01:17:37.240 --> 01:17:40.253
So if you're a developing
country, you connect with China,

01:17:41.210 --> 01:17:43.240
all of a sudden you're
connecting with a competitor

01:17:43.240 --> 01:17:47.610
rather than a country to which
you can send your exports.

01:17:47.610 --> 01:17:50.260
So, the reason I think this
is important is when you,

01:17:51.130 --> 01:17:55.100
I'll skip that because
there's no time left

01:17:55.100 --> 01:17:56.870
but I'll tell you very briefly

01:17:56.870 --> 01:18:00.050
what eventually our
empirical results show.

01:18:00.050 --> 01:18:04.290
The empirical results
show that the market size

01:18:04.290 --> 01:18:07.740
is very important for
expanding poverty reduction.

01:18:07.740 --> 01:18:12.710
And market size, as I said,
is defined by three variables.

01:18:12.710 --> 01:18:15.940
So the population, the total
population is important.

01:18:15.940 --> 01:18:17.930
International integration is important.

01:18:17.930 --> 01:18:21.130
And when we look at international
integration, actually,

01:18:21.130 --> 01:18:24.140
what's most important is
how rich the countries

01:18:24.140 --> 01:18:25.600
you connect with are.

01:18:25.600 --> 01:18:29.930
So, connecting two countries
with higher relative income

01:18:29.930 --> 01:18:34.060
is much more valuable than
connecting to countries

01:18:34.060 --> 01:18:35.410
with large populations.

01:18:35.410 --> 01:18:37.013
That's what the results suggest.

01:18:37.013 --> 01:18:39.150
Suggest to give you some numbers.

01:18:39.150 --> 01:18:40.560
If you connect with a country

01:18:40.560 --> 01:18:45.000
that has the same relative
population, as you do on average,

01:18:45.000 --> 01:18:49.293
it's like adding 160,000
people to your country.

01:18:49.293 --> 01:18:51.652
If you connect with a country

01:18:51.652 --> 01:18:54.640
that has the same
relative income as you do,

01:18:54.640 --> 01:18:56.420
then it's like connected,

01:18:56.420 --> 01:18:59.880
it's like adding 20 million
people to your population.

01:18:59.880 --> 01:19:04.650
So the income, the relative
income is much more important.

01:19:04.650 --> 01:19:06.170
And this has direct implications.

01:19:06.170 --> 01:19:08.870
So to give you an example,
Kenya is a country

01:19:08.870 --> 01:19:10.600
that right now is considering,

01:19:10.600 --> 01:19:13.280
should we engage in a bilateral agreement

01:19:13.280 --> 01:19:15.630
with the United States?

01:19:15.630 --> 01:19:17.460
Or should we be focused more

01:19:17.460 --> 01:19:19.990
in a pan-African trade agreement?

01:19:19.990 --> 01:19:21.750
If you take these results,

01:19:21.750 --> 01:19:23.810
they suggest it's much
more valuable for Kenya

01:19:23.810 --> 01:19:27.070
to connect to the United
States, which is a rich economy,

01:19:27.070 --> 01:19:30.400
rather than relying on the African market.

01:19:30.400 --> 01:19:31.580
Again, this is not surprising.

01:19:31.580 --> 01:19:33.860
This is what Korea did and
this is what China did.

01:19:33.860 --> 01:19:36.360
And this is what the results
of the model also say,

01:19:36.360 --> 01:19:39.489
actually quantitative terms,
they suggest huge effects.

01:19:39.489 --> 01:19:42.040
Now, of course, the problem
is that it's not clear

01:19:42.040 --> 01:19:43.380
that all these countries can connect

01:19:43.380 --> 01:19:44.940
to the United States anymore, right?

01:19:44.940 --> 01:19:46.820
So so what we're saying
is if you cannot connect

01:19:46.820 --> 01:19:49.190
to the United States,
maybe the alternative

01:19:49.190 --> 01:19:52.750
is to connect to your neighbors
that may not be as rich

01:19:52.750 --> 01:19:54.430
but they have a very large population,

01:19:54.430 --> 01:19:57.670
and this is not as viable,
but it's still viable

01:19:57.670 --> 01:19:59.740
compared to the alternative
of being completely closed.

01:19:59.740 --> 01:20:01.450
So you can take these
results as giving you

01:20:01.450 --> 01:20:04.500
some sense of how important
regional integration may be

01:20:04.500 --> 01:20:06.530
in that setting.

01:20:06.530 --> 01:20:08.500
But finally, and I want to stop with that

01:20:08.500 --> 01:20:11.730
because this is more in
the spirit of Kuznets.

01:20:11.730 --> 01:20:13.810
Finally, we consider one counterfactual,

01:20:13.810 --> 01:20:15.790
which is the strongest one where we say,

01:20:15.790 --> 01:20:17.950
suppose there is no integration at all.

01:20:17.950 --> 01:20:21.630
So not only can't you connect
with the United States,

01:20:21.630 --> 01:20:24.430
but you cannot even connect
with your neighbors.

01:20:24.430 --> 01:20:26.180
And this is not so far fetched,

01:20:26.180 --> 01:20:29.704
because many developing
countries view the neighbors

01:20:29.704 --> 01:20:33.836
as big competitors, so they're
not willing to engage in,

01:20:33.836 --> 01:20:38.470
in trade cooperation.

01:20:38.470 --> 01:20:41.410
And so if you just rely
on the domestic market,

01:20:41.410 --> 01:20:44.370
how large does this
domestic market have to be

01:20:44.370 --> 01:20:48.290
so that you can have sustained
poverty reduction, okay?

01:20:48.290 --> 01:20:50.084
And so the number we get out of,

01:20:50.084 --> 01:20:53.760
this is the threshold is 328 million.

01:20:53.760 --> 01:20:57.810
So these are 3328 million enrolled bodies,

01:20:57.810 --> 01:21:00.990
so people that are below
the middle class, okay?

01:21:00.990 --> 01:21:05.510
So think of those as being
328 million poor people.

01:21:05.510 --> 01:21:08.241
So, the only country
that meets these criteria

01:21:08.241 --> 01:21:10.070
is India, right?

01:21:10.070 --> 01:21:11.870
There is no one else.

01:21:11.870 --> 01:21:16.870
And this is something that actually

01:21:17.110 --> 01:21:17.943
the graph shows.

01:21:18.910 --> 01:21:23.910
Now, the reason this is not so bleak is,

01:21:24.909 --> 01:21:29.610
if you don't have 328 million raw bodies,

01:21:29.610 --> 01:21:33.550
you can still meet this
threshold by increasing,

01:21:33.550 --> 01:21:37.170
by having a middle class by
increasing the purchasing power

01:21:37.170 --> 01:21:38.880
of the people within your country.

01:21:38.880 --> 01:21:43.880
So this is one way of
transforming a raw body

01:21:44.960 --> 01:21:47.540
to someone who can actually consume more.

01:21:47.540 --> 01:21:50.370
And so, again, to give
you a specific example,

01:21:50.370 --> 01:21:52.010
take the case of Norway.

01:21:52.010 --> 01:21:57.010
Norway found oil in the late 1960s.

01:21:59.780 --> 01:22:03.130
At that point already Norway
was a fairly equal society.

01:22:03.130 --> 01:22:04.360
It's a very small country.

01:22:04.360 --> 01:22:08.133
It's not a country that has
relied on trade to grow,

01:22:09.650 --> 01:22:12.300
it's not a country that has
a very large domestic market,

01:22:12.300 --> 01:22:15.410
it's not a country that has
a very large population.

01:22:15.410 --> 01:22:18.330
But it's a country that has always had

01:22:18.330 --> 01:22:20.750
a very high degree of equality.

01:22:20.750 --> 01:22:23.730
I forget what the exact
population of Norway is.

01:22:23.730 --> 01:22:25.690
It's certainly not 300 million.

01:22:25.690 --> 01:22:30.657
But if you actually,
according to our estimates,

01:22:30.657 --> 01:22:34.800
if you go from zero
percent, that's middle class

01:22:34.800 --> 01:22:39.650
to 100% middle class,
in the average country,

01:22:39.650 --> 01:22:42.930
this is like adding 540 million people.

01:22:42.930 --> 01:22:46.860
So if your middle class increases by 10%,

01:22:46.860 --> 01:22:51.360
then it's like adding 54
poor people in the country.

01:22:51.360 --> 01:22:56.040
So, ultimately, what
this suggests is that if

01:22:57.300 --> 01:23:01.450
we end up in a world where
we have much less trade

01:23:01.450 --> 01:23:03.100
and much less regional integration,

01:23:03.100 --> 01:23:07.830
so with open trade, it's still
going to be the first choice.

01:23:07.830 --> 01:23:10.530
It's extremely important
for developing countries.

01:23:10.530 --> 01:23:12.630
And the smaller the country,
the more important it is.

01:23:12.630 --> 01:23:15.560
But if that's not an option,
if you have to rely more

01:23:15.560 --> 01:23:18.770
on the domestic market,
what are the options

01:23:19.830 --> 01:23:24.830
not to have war in order to race,

01:23:25.860 --> 01:23:28.210
to enlarge your country?

01:23:28.210 --> 01:23:30.280
You may argue, some might argue,

01:23:30.280 --> 01:23:31.980
you may want higher fertility

01:23:31.980 --> 01:23:33.470
to increase your population.

01:23:33.470 --> 01:23:38.470
This would be a very perverse
way to justify all this,

01:23:39.470 --> 01:23:41.130
sometimes we have this policy to try

01:23:41.130 --> 01:23:42.720
to actually increase population.

01:23:42.720 --> 01:23:47.290
Tanzania is one example, where
there is a very strong policy

01:23:47.290 --> 01:23:49.750
where the current head of the state

01:23:49.750 --> 01:23:52.260
is encouraging people to have more kids.

01:23:52.260 --> 01:23:54.600
But of course, this creates
all kinds of other problems.

01:23:54.600 --> 01:23:56.950
So if you're not going
to advocate any of these,

01:23:56.950 --> 01:24:01.170
then an alternative is to try
to create a more equal society

01:24:01.170 --> 01:24:04.250
with larger middle class,
because by adding middle class

01:24:04.250 --> 01:24:06.260
you add purchasing power.

01:24:06.260 --> 01:24:08.610
And again, this is not something new here.

01:24:08.610 --> 01:24:10.810
Many people have made the point before

01:24:10.810 --> 01:24:12.820
that the middle class may be important,

01:24:12.820 --> 01:24:14.250
but here using this framework,

01:24:14.250 --> 01:24:18.070
we can actually quantify
how important this is.

01:24:18.070 --> 01:24:22.360
And finally, how do you
create a middle class?

01:24:22.360 --> 01:24:24.430
So, again, in this model that we have,

01:24:24.430 --> 01:24:26.300
there is a very direct mapping

01:24:26.300 --> 01:24:28.494
between the theoretical structure

01:24:28.494 --> 01:24:32.450
and the policy implications,
which is the way you create it

01:24:32.450 --> 01:24:36.840
is to sort of extend by taxing
income, but more importantly,

01:24:36.840 --> 01:24:40.970
by a more equitable
distribution of shares.

01:24:40.970 --> 01:24:45.700
So, it actually, it's an argument in favor

01:24:45.700 --> 01:24:49.761
of wealth redistribution, more
than income redistribution.

01:24:49.761 --> 01:24:52.380
And I will stop here
but I want to make sure

01:24:52.380 --> 01:24:55.060
that this last comment is not interpreted

01:24:55.060 --> 01:24:58.210
as advocacy for the wealth tax.

01:24:58.210 --> 01:25:01.550
I want to make clear
that this is an argument

01:25:01.550 --> 01:25:04.617
that I think may be very
relevant in very poor countries,

01:25:04.617 --> 01:25:07.060
countries like Angola or Nigeria,

01:25:07.060 --> 01:25:10.810
where you have a very large
share of the population

01:25:10.810 --> 01:25:12.400
owning next to nothing.

01:25:12.400 --> 01:25:14.560
And in this country,
there is a certain degree

01:25:14.560 --> 01:25:18.160
of redistribution from the
rich to the middle class,

01:25:18.160 --> 01:25:21.930
but also to the poor may
ultimately be a precondition

01:25:21.930 --> 01:25:24.840
for growth, rather than
an impediment to growth.

01:25:24.840 --> 01:25:27.332
So I will stop here, thank you
very much for your patience.

01:25:27.332 --> 01:25:30.499
(audience applauding)

01:25:37.928 --> 01:25:38.761
- So we're a bit short on time

01:25:38.761 --> 01:25:41.253
but we'll take a couple of questions.

01:25:41.253 --> 01:25:44.003
(speaks off-mic)

01:25:59.013 --> 01:26:01.320
- Thank you very much for this diverse

01:26:01.320 --> 01:26:03.640
and insightful lecture.

01:26:03.640 --> 01:26:06.650
I have two questions,
but feel free to shorten

01:26:06.650 --> 01:26:09.359
if you want to take more.

01:26:09.359 --> 01:26:12.830
One is that in the beginning,
you said, trade helped growth

01:26:12.830 --> 01:26:15.860
in Asia very much, but not so
much in Sub Saharan Africa.

01:26:15.860 --> 01:26:19.010
And I was interested in what you think

01:26:19.010 --> 01:26:23.220
are the most convincing
explanations for that phenomenon?

01:26:23.220 --> 01:26:26.300
And secondly, when we
argue that we should invest

01:26:26.300 --> 01:26:30.083
in a global middle class,
how do you think will that,

01:26:31.790 --> 01:26:35.980
might that be an issue
with current debates

01:26:35.980 --> 01:26:40.590
on the limits to that sort
of consumption globally

01:26:40.590 --> 01:26:43.683
with regards to environmental concerns?

01:26:48.645 --> 01:26:53.645
- I'll take three questions and then...

01:26:56.243 --> 01:26:58.500
- Welcome back, thank
you for being with us.

01:26:58.500 --> 01:27:00.810
I just had a question, speaking
about the elephant curves,

01:27:00.810 --> 01:27:03.453
dragon curves, more inequality.

01:27:04.810 --> 01:27:07.580
Also, there has been very
much, much lower confidence

01:27:07.580 --> 01:27:08.960
in the Bretton Woods institutions.

01:27:08.960 --> 01:27:11.630
And my question is referring
to the Andersen et al paper,

01:27:11.630 --> 01:27:15.670
which was published on the
World Bank website 10 days ago

01:27:15.670 --> 01:27:18.673
and speaking about this whole
idea of foreign aid capture.

01:27:20.431 --> 01:27:24.180
My question is, what do
you think about that paper?

01:27:24.180 --> 01:27:27.030
I thought it was stunning that it showed

01:27:27.030 --> 01:27:31.240
all those correlations between
World Bank, dispersions

01:27:31.240 --> 01:27:36.240
and higher levels of
offshore capital accounts

01:27:36.310 --> 01:27:37.890
by like seven and a half percent.

01:27:37.890 --> 01:27:39.050
What's your view towards that?

01:27:39.050 --> 01:27:41.370
I also totally understand
that if your hand is tied

01:27:41.370 --> 01:27:43.793
for the next 72 hours, thank you.

01:27:52.240 --> 01:27:55.439
- Okay, so let me answer these
questions if there are more.

01:27:55.439 --> 01:27:57.733
So regarding the role of trade.

01:27:59.330 --> 01:28:04.330
So Africa has not traded much
with the rest of the world

01:28:04.420 --> 01:28:06.290
the way East Asia had.

01:28:06.290 --> 01:28:08.620
So it's not that trade
did not help Africa,

01:28:08.620 --> 01:28:11.990
is that trade has not happened in Africa

01:28:11.990 --> 01:28:14.742
the way it has happened in East Asia

01:28:14.742 --> 01:28:17.810
to the extent that trade
has been present in Africa.

01:28:17.810 --> 01:28:19.700
For the most part, it has been in the form

01:28:19.700 --> 01:28:21.480
of commodity exports.

01:28:21.480 --> 01:28:24.360
And I think that that is very,

01:28:24.360 --> 01:28:26.770
the story there is very
consistent with the one

01:28:26.770 --> 01:28:28.080
I just presented.

01:28:28.080 --> 01:28:30.730
Namely, you have commodity exports,

01:28:30.730 --> 01:28:33.330
they generate export revenue.

01:28:33.330 --> 01:28:36.850
But the growth these
export revenues create

01:28:36.850 --> 01:28:39.140
never trickles down,
it doesn't trickle down

01:28:39.140 --> 01:28:41.810
because it stays in the hands of very few.

01:28:41.810 --> 01:28:45.210
That's certainly the story
that you hear for Nigeria

01:28:45.210 --> 01:28:47.100
or Angola, but many other countries,

01:28:47.100 --> 01:28:49.524
that it's not growth that trickles down.

01:28:49.524 --> 01:28:52.280
In Asia, it was a very different story

01:28:52.280 --> 01:28:56.860
where trade not only took
advantage of the low wage labor

01:28:58.230 --> 01:29:01.510
in China or Korea or
Taiwan and they, of course,

01:29:01.510 --> 01:29:03.600
also took advantage of the rich markets

01:29:03.600 --> 01:29:08.483
in the United States and
Europe but in addition to that,

01:29:09.400 --> 01:29:14.000
the resources they amassed
through trade or exports

01:29:14.000 --> 01:29:15.920
were invested very differently
in these countries.

01:29:15.920 --> 01:29:18.510
They were investing to
fund infrastructure,

01:29:18.510 --> 01:29:20.410
substantial physical infrastructure,

01:29:20.410 --> 01:29:22.800
but also human capital infrastructure.

01:29:22.800 --> 01:29:26.070
So they did make these investments
that were very important

01:29:26.070 --> 01:29:28.950
for growth and ultimately,
poverty reduction.

01:29:28.950 --> 01:29:31.800
So the developing experiences,
two parts of the world

01:29:31.800 --> 01:29:33.153
were very, very different.

01:29:36.419 --> 01:29:38.123
I'm sorry, the second question was?

01:29:39.519 --> 01:29:41.240
On consumption.

01:29:41.240 --> 01:29:43.573
Ah, that's a good question.

01:29:48.020 --> 01:29:49.510
So I'll tell you my view.

01:29:49.510 --> 01:29:52.580
So when no one talks
about trade these days,

01:29:52.580 --> 01:29:57.580
and especially, trade as it
relates to the middle class

01:29:57.767 --> 01:29:59.390
and the consumption of the middle class,

01:29:59.390 --> 01:30:01.310
there's immediately push back.

01:30:01.310 --> 01:30:04.690
Because if developing
countries grow the same way

01:30:04.690 --> 01:30:07.540
that developed countries
have grown in the past,

01:30:07.540 --> 01:30:10.630
this is going to put a lot of
pressure on the environment.

01:30:10.630 --> 01:30:12.460
And that's for sure.

01:30:12.460 --> 01:30:13.890
Two thoughts on these.

01:30:13.890 --> 01:30:16.680
First, if you actually look at where

01:30:16.680 --> 01:30:18.917
the most of the pollution is,

01:30:18.917 --> 01:30:22.770
it's concentrated on some very
large developing countries,

01:30:22.770 --> 01:30:25.820
mostly China and India.

01:30:25.820 --> 01:30:30.780
So one could gain a lot, one
could make great progress

01:30:30.780 --> 01:30:31.960
in addressing these issues

01:30:31.960 --> 01:30:34.107
just by focusing on these two countries.

01:30:34.107 --> 01:30:37.060
And China is by no means,
it's not a developed country,

01:30:37.060 --> 01:30:41.450
but it's by no means a
country that cannot make

01:30:41.450 --> 01:30:44.000
a serious effort in
addressing these issues.

01:30:44.000 --> 01:30:46.870
I think when it comes to
countries in Sub Saharan Africa,

01:30:46.870 --> 01:30:51.110
so countries like still
Kenya or Ethiopia or Mali,

01:30:51.110 --> 01:30:54.010
or Chad, countries where the poverty rates

01:30:54.010 --> 01:30:55.510
are extremely high still.

01:30:55.510 --> 01:30:58.050
Where you have 30% of the population

01:30:58.050 --> 01:30:59.740
living under the poverty rate,

01:30:59.740 --> 01:31:02.590
or when you have a country like Niger

01:31:02.590 --> 01:31:04.970
where you have the median
age of the population

01:31:04.970 --> 01:31:07.650
is 15 years, 15 years.

01:31:07.650 --> 01:31:09.270
Imagine what this means.

01:31:09.270 --> 01:31:12.370
Then in my view, climate
change is not a priority

01:31:12.370 --> 01:31:14.060
in this country.

01:31:14.060 --> 01:31:15.723
So when you go there and you say,

01:31:17.440 --> 01:31:21.383
developing a middle
class or growth or trade

01:31:21.383 --> 01:31:24.100
is not that important, you
should have second thoughts

01:31:24.100 --> 01:31:27.030
about all these because it's
bad for the environment.

01:31:27.030 --> 01:31:29.150
I mean, not only do I think
this is not effective,

01:31:29.150 --> 01:31:31.960
I think it's more for us to be saying that

01:31:31.960 --> 01:31:34.290
to these countries when
we don't do our part.

01:31:34.290 --> 01:31:37.360
So I think we should
start with, of course,

01:31:37.360 --> 01:31:39.100
the advanced economies to the extent

01:31:39.100 --> 01:31:41.920
that you go to developing
countries, China and India

01:31:41.920 --> 01:31:44.240
before we go to sub Saharan Africa

01:31:44.240 --> 01:31:46.573
and talk about climate change there.

01:31:47.890 --> 01:31:50.030
Regarding the elite capture paper,

01:31:50.030 --> 01:31:51.910
so I don't want to talk
about this paper simply

01:31:51.910 --> 01:31:55.630
because this is not the
topic of the Kuznets lecture.

01:31:55.630 --> 01:31:58.960
I mean, institutions are
important for development,

01:31:58.960 --> 01:31:59.963
that's for sure.

01:32:01.220 --> 01:32:03.260
In the framework I just
showed you institutions

01:32:03.260 --> 01:32:06.880
are indirectly measured
by the fixed costs.

01:32:06.880 --> 01:32:09.560
And we do find that they play

01:32:09.560 --> 01:32:11.840
a very important role in development.

01:32:11.840 --> 01:32:15.180
I mean, one issue that
not just the World Bank,

01:32:15.180 --> 01:32:17.830
the development community has always faced

01:32:17.830 --> 01:32:18.950
is when it comes to development.

01:32:18.950 --> 01:32:21.030
Everyone always says
institutions are important,

01:32:21.030 --> 01:32:24.270
you can't make progress
unless you fix institutions.

01:32:24.270 --> 01:32:26.933
But no one as a recipe of
how to fix institutions.

01:32:28.240 --> 01:32:29.943
What is the right policy?

01:32:32.018 --> 01:32:33.550
How do you do better?

01:32:33.550 --> 01:32:35.360
I think what research has shown

01:32:35.360 --> 01:32:40.270
that perhaps the only constructive
insight in that regard

01:32:40.270 --> 01:32:43.340
is the only thing that
helps is transparency.

01:32:43.340 --> 01:32:47.000
I do think that the
World Bank should follow

01:32:47.000 --> 01:32:52.000
the same policies it advises
other countries to follow

01:32:53.890 --> 01:32:56.160
namely be completely transparent.

01:32:56.160 --> 01:32:58.750
Regarding this paper, I'm
gonna just make one comment.

01:32:58.750 --> 01:33:00.827
If you actually look at the
aid affected by all this,

01:33:00.827 --> 01:33:05.520
it's a very small percentage, it's 7%.

01:33:05.520 --> 01:33:09.080
So whatever the paper is,
I don't think it speaks

01:33:09.080 --> 01:33:12.053
to a big issue in development.

01:33:14.118 --> 01:33:16.546
(Rohini speaking off-mic)

01:33:16.546 --> 01:33:19.713
(audience applauding)

01:33:28.445 --> 01:33:31.195
(closing music)

